WEBVTT

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:29.160
<v Ira Glass>OK-- this happens to be Chicago, but every city has a place like this. That weird, desolate area at the far end of town. We're a half mile west of the old abandoned steel mills. We're a half mile north of landfills where methane fires used to burn. Just south of the auto junkyard. Just east of the site of the old city dump, where there was a mountain of raw garbage that would stink up the neighborhood whenever the wind would blow in the wrong direction.
<v Ira Glass>好吧——这里恰好是芝加哥，但每个城市都有这样一个地方。就是城郊尽头那个诡异、荒凉的区域。我们在老废弃钢铁厂以西半英里处。我们在过去常年燃烧沼气火灾的垃圾填埋场以北半英里处。就在汽车垃圾场的正南边。就在老城市垃圾倾倒场遗址的正东边，那里有一座成山的未经处理的垃圾，每当风向不对时，就会把整个街区熏得臭气熏天。

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:33.160
<v Ira Glass>Everybody down here called it Mount Pacini for the alderman who let the city put it here.
<v Ira Glass>这里的每个人都把它叫作帕奇尼山（Mount Pacini），因为那位议员允许市里把垃圾倒在这里。

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:43.790
<v Charlie Gregerson>You'll notice all these-- what would you call it-- tire marks. This street is used as-- for drag racing year-round.
<v Charlie Gregerson>你会注意到所有这些——你会怎么称呼它呢——轮胎印。这条街一年到头都被用来赛车。

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:45.140
<v Ira Glass>Really?
<v Ira Glass>真的吗？

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:50.170
<v Charlie Gregerson>Yeah. Because it's basically far enough away from the police that they don't do anything about it.
<v Charlie Gregerson>对。因为这里离警察局足够远，他们基本上不管这事。

00:00:50.170 --> 00:01:13.700
<v Ira Glass>My guide is Charlie Gregerson, who grew up down here. He shows me where a lake-- Lake Calumet-- used to be back in the '40s when he was a kid. He'd go fishing on a rowboat with his dad. Then the city started filling in huge sections of the lake with garbage and incinerator ash. He'd come here in the '70s and see bulldozers pushing around the rubble of some of Chicago's great buildings, which had been recently demolished.
<v Ira Glass>我的向导是查理·格雷格森（Charlie Gregerson），他在这一带长大。他带我去看40年代他还是个孩子的时候，一个湖——卡柳梅特湖（Lake Calumet）——曾经所在的地方。那时他会和爸爸一起坐划艇去钓鱼。后来，市里开始用垃圾和焚烧炉灰烬填满湖泊的巨大区域。70年代他来到这里时，会看到推土机在推平芝加哥一些不久前刚被拆毁的宏伟建筑的废墟。

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:18.830
<v Ira Glass>Louis Sullivan masterpieces, like the stock exchange building and the Garrick Theater-- this is where they ended up.
<v Ira Glass>路易斯·沙利文（Louis Sullivan）的杰作，比如证券交易所大楼和加里克剧院（Garrick Theater）——这就是它们的最终归宿。

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:24.030
<v Ira Glass>Now, show me-- we're standing here-- where were all the buildings being dumped, and what did that look like?
<v Ira Glass>现在，告诉我——我们正站在这里——所有这些建筑都是被倒在哪里的？那是个什么景象？

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:45.780
<v Charlie Gregerson>Right here. Right here at what was the north end of the dump. And actually, we picked-- I picked up a few pieces of the stock exchange ornament right out of the lake. But of course, most of it had been ground right into the dirt, because they had bulldozers that would just-- they'd keep on-- they would dump the stuff in piles, and the bulldozer would just flatten it all out.
<v Charlie Gregerson>就在这儿。就在垃圾场北端的这儿。实际上，我们挑了——我直接从湖里捡了几块证券交易所的装饰构件。但当然，大部分都已经被碾碎进泥土里了，因为当时有推土机——它们会一直作业——它们把东西堆成堆，然后推土机直接把它们全部平整掉。

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:50.240
<v Ira Glass>And so there'd be this, like, Louis Sullivan, you know, terracotta ornament just sticking out?
<v Ira Glass>所以就会有像路易斯·沙利文的陶土装饰品那样，你知道的，就那么突出来？

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.540
<v Charlie Gregerson>Yeah, just laying out there. Yeah.
<v Charlie Gregerson>对，就躺在那儿。对。

00:01:52.540 --> 00:02:01.270
<v Ira Glass>And so walking around when there's these pieces of building sticking up, I mean, it just seems like it just must have been such a strange scene. Like this apocalyptic, you know, death of a city--
<v Ira Glass>所以四处走动时，看到这些建筑的碎片凸现出来，我的意思是，这感觉一定是个极其诡异的场景。就像这种末日般的，你知道的，城市的死亡——

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:27.640
<v Charlie Gregerson>Oh, yeah. Well, there were-- I remember seeing one of these big Phoenix columns that I knew had come out of the Garrick Theater, was just sticking out of the ground. Two of those in the Garrick Theater distributed the weight of the upper floors that were over the stage. One of those was just sticking out right about a 45-degree angle out of the ground. And at that point, the Garrick had been gone for almost 10 years.
<v Charlie Gregerson>哦，对。嗯，有——我记得看到过一根巨大的菲尼克斯柱（Phoenix column），我知道它是从加里克剧院拆下来的，就那么从地面突出来。加里克剧院里有两根这样的柱子，用来支撑舞台上方高层楼层的重量。其中一根就以大约45度的角度从地面斜伸出来。而当时，加里克剧院已经消失快10年了。

00:02:27.640 --> 00:03:11.510
<v Ira         Glass>There were once big plans for this area-- for canals and waterways, a harbor that never really worked out. There are zoning maps of the city that show streets and complete neighborhoods -- a whole grid of them-- that nobody ever got around to building. Instead, now, on top of all the trash stands a golf course. Charlie says that from the clubhouse, he gets exactly the same view that he used to get back when he and his dad took out the rowboat. It's the same spot. That's where the lake once was. You can see clear to downtown. So far away, it might as well be another city.
<v Ira         Glass>这里曾经有过宏大的规划——要修建运河和水道，还有一个最终未能成型的港口。市里的区划图上显示着街道和完整的街区——一整片规划好的网格——但从来没有人真正去建设它们。相反，现在，所有的垃圾上面座落着一座高尔夫球场。查理说，从俱乐部会所望出去，他看到的景色和他当年跟父亲划着小船出去时一模一样。就是同一个地方。那是曾经湖泊所在的位置。你可以一直看到市中心。离得那么远，它完全可以算作是另一个城市了。

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:30.210
<v Ira         Glass>Well today on our program, we have stories from several places like this, in the shadow of the city. That weird, no man's land, where it always feels like secret stuff is happening, you know? Just out of sight. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Our program today was first broadcast a few years ago. It's in three acts.
<v Ira         Glass>今天在我们的节目中，我们将讲述几个这样地方的故事，它们隐藏在城市的阴影里。那种诡异的、无人问津的土地，总让人觉得有什么秘密的事情正在发生，你知道吗？就在视线之外。来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这里是《此地美国》（This American Life）。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。我们今天的节目最初是在几年前广播的。它分为三个幕。

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:46.130
<v Ira         Glass>Act One, Brooklyn Archipelago. In that act, some passengers set sail one day on a three-hour tour-- a three-hour tour-- and end up getting lost in the wilderness. One fears for his life on a string of islands that is just outside a very, very big city.
<v Ira         Glass>第一幕，布鲁克林群岛（Brooklyn Archipelago）。在这一幕中，几位乘客有一天登上一艘游船，开始了一次三小时的旅行——一次三小时的旅行——结果在荒野中迷失了方向。一个人在一个紧挨着一个超级大城市的群岛上，为自己的生命感到担忧。

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:53.560
<v Ira         Glass>Act Two, Troubled Bridge Over Water. A guy goes to a remote spot to help people who do not want to be helped.
<v Ira         Glass>第二幕，激流中的险桥（Troubled Bridge Over Water）。一个人去了一个偏远的地方，去帮助那些根本不需要帮助的人。

00:03:53.560 --> 00:04:12.440
<v Ira         Glass>Act Three, Please In My Backyard. Controversy over industrial odors coming from a factory. Odors that, for once, people want to keep coming. Stay with us.
<v Ira         Glass>第三幕，请建在我家后院（Please In My Backyard）。围绕一家工厂散发的工业气味产生了争议。这种气味，破天荒地，人们竟然希望它继续散发出来。请继续收听。

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:27.680
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Brooklyn Archipelago. Brett Martin has this story which takes place on the outskirts of-- well, perhaps you've already figured out which city.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，布鲁克林群岛。布雷特·马丁（Brett Martin）带来了这个故事，故事发生在——嗯，也许你已经猜到是哪个城市了郊外。

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:46.550
<v Brett Martin>Listen, it happens. You go out for a night with your friends and you wind up drunk, in your underwear, soaking wet, covered with blood, and shipwrecked on a desert island, all within sight of the Empire State Building. These things happen. Or at least they did happen to Alex Zharov.
<v Brett Martin>听着，这种事会发生。你和朋友出去玩了一夜，结果喝得酩酊大醉，只穿着内衣，全身湿透，浑身是血，在一座荒岛上遭遇海难，而这一切都在帝国大厦的视野之内。这种事是会发生的。或者至少亚历山大·扎罗夫（Alex Zharov）确实碰到了。

00:04:46.550 --> 00:05:05.220
<v Brett Martin>Alex is 17 years old. He moved to the US from a small town in the Ukraine when he was nine. He's skinny and wears tie-dyed T-shirts, an unmanageable spray of frizzy blond hair, and a valiant-- if not altogether successful-- starter mustache. And-- well, he can probably introduce himself better than I can. Here's how he responds when I ask him to state his name for the record.
<v Brett Martin>亚历山大17岁。他9岁时和家人从乌克兰的一个小镇移民到美国。他身材瘦削，穿着扎染T恤，一头怎么也理不顺的卷曲金发，还留着一撇英勇的——即使算不上多成功的——初级胡须。而且——嗯，他可能比我更能自我介绍。当我请他正式报出自己的名字时，他是这样回答的。

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:19.450
<v Alex Zharov>My name is Alex Zharov, and I love to have very radical experiences in life. And I consider myself to be a psychedelic artistically productive person.
<v Alex Zharov>我叫亚历山大·扎罗夫，我喜欢在生活中体验非常激进的经历。我认为自己是一个具有艺术产出力的迷幻主义者。

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:40.330
<v Brett Martin>Here are a few other things about Alex. He lives with his cute older girlfriend and his exceptionally patient parents in a small apartment in the Midwood section of Brooklyn. Instead of going to high school, he's enrolled in an internet home schooling program. He's at work on a science fiction novel and has logged several hundred in-flight hours as a student pilot.
<v Brett Martin>关于亚历山大，还有其他几件事。他和可爱的年长女友以及极其耐心的父母住在布鲁克林米德伍德（Midwood）区的一间小公寓里。他没有去上高中，而是注册了一个互联网家庭学校项目。他正在写一部科幻小说，并以学生飞行员的身份积累了数百个飞行小时。

00:05:40.330 --> 00:06:13.890
<v Brett Martin>But most of Alex's time is spent as a guitarist, singer, and songwriter for his band, EBuffalo. When I went to see them play at a two-day Russian rock festival last fall, I learned several things. First, there are many, many ex-Soviet immigrants living in Brooklyn. Second, they all, very earnestly, want to rock. And third, Alex Zharov-- whether he's writhing on his back on stage or reclining in the dressing room with a beer and a cigarette-- is kind of a superstar.
<v Brett Martin>但亚历山大的大部分时间都是作为他的乐队EBuffalo的吉他手、主唱和词曲作者度过的。去年秋天，当我去观看他们在为一个为期两天的俄罗斯摇滚节演出时，我了解到了几件事。首先，有非常多的前苏联移民住在布鲁克林。其次，他们所有人都非常认真地想要摇滚。第三，亚历山大·扎罗夫——无论他是在舞台上痛苦地扭动身体，还是在化妆室里叼着烟喝着啤酒斜靠着——都有点像个超级巨星。

00:06:13.890 --> 00:06:38.780
<v Brett Martin>Before we get to our story, the other key person you'll need to meet is someone who entered Alex's life at a crucial moment years ago when Alex first came to the States. Alex had an awkward adjustment. He fought in school and was kind of depressed. He was bored. Then one day, Alex was walking along the Brighton Beach boardwalk and saw a group of older guys collecting money for something called the Russian Punk Rock Club of America. Older guys, like 25 and 30 years old. Alex was 12.
<v Brett Martin>在我们讲到我们的故事之前，你需要认识的另一个关键人物是在几年前亚历山大刚到美国时的一个关键时刻闯入他生活的人。亚历山大当时很不适应。他在学校打架，而且有点沮丧。他很无聊。直到有一天，亚历山大沿着布莱顿海滩（Brighton Beach）的木板路散步，看到一群大龄青年在为一个叫作“美国俄罗斯朋克摇滚俱乐部”的组织筹钱。大龄青年，大概25、30岁左右。亚历山大当时12岁。

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:42.600
<v Brett Martin>One of the musicians he met that day was Roman Gadzhilov, who immediately took to the young Alex.
<v Brett Martin>他那天遇到的其中一位音乐家是罗曼·加吉洛夫（Roman Gadzhilov），他立刻对年幼的亚历山大产生了好感。

00:06:42.600 --> 00:07:04.140
<v Roman Gadzhilov>Well, he had this blink in his eyes. It's sometimes you see extraordinary person, and, you know, you kind of know this. You know, he wasn't appeared to us as 12-year-old at that moment. At 12 years old, he was writing songs that I was writing at 18. And after this, we've been together all the time.
<v Roman Gadzhilov>嗯，他的眼睛里闪烁着光芒。有时候你会看到一个非凡的人，你知道的，你心里就会有这种感觉。你知道的，在那一刻，他给我们的感觉不像个12岁的孩子。在12岁的时候，他写的歌我已经到了18岁才写得出来。在这之后，我们一直都在一起。

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:05.760
<v Roman Gadzhilov>We called him Khryusha you know, that's--
<v Roman Gadzhilov>我们叫他赫留沙（Khryusha），你知道的，那是——

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:08.190
<v Brett Martin>And what does that mean?
<v Brett Martin>那是什么意思？

00:07:08.190 --> 00:07:12.070
<v Roman Gadzhilov>Khryusha means [LAUGHING] little piglet. Little piglet.
<v Roman Gadzhilov>[笑]赫留沙意思是小猪。小猪。

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:33.440
<v Brett Martin>Under his new friends' tutelage, Alex began walking around in an old Bolshevik-style hat and trench coat. And his friends gave him books. Dostoevsky, Tolkien, guides to Slavic paganism, The Beats, and also Robinson Crusoe and Treasure Island. Alex was particularly fond of those.
<v Brett Martin>在新朋友的指导下，亚历山大开始穿着一顶老式布尔什维克风格的帽子和风衣四处走动。他的朋友们还送了他书。陀思妥耶夫斯基、托尔金、《斯拉夫异教指南》、《垮掉的一代》，还有《鲁滨逊漂流记》和《金银岛》。亚历山大特别喜欢这些书。

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:53.410
<v Brett Martin>In our story today, our own seafaring tale happens on a boat that Roman owns-- a 25-foot white sailboat, which Alex likes to refer to as "the yacht." One cool evening last May, Alex, Roman, and another friend named Alex-- Alex Glubochansky decided to take a nice little boat trip in Jamaica Bay, the body of water that wraps around the southern end of Brooklyn. Here's Alex.
<v Brett Martin>在我们今天的这个故事中，我们的航海故事发生在罗曼拥有的一艘船上——一艘25英尺长的白色帆船，亚历山大喜欢称之为“游艇”。去年5月的一个凉爽的傍晚，亚历山大、罗曼和另一个名叫亚历山大的朋友——亚历山大·格鲁博尚斯基（Alex Glubochansky）决定在牙买加湾（Jamaica Bay）进行一次愉快的小船游，这片水域环绕着布鲁克林的南端。这是亚历山大在说话。

00:07:53.410 --> 00:08:12.320
<v Alex Zharov>The three of us decided to just get, like, 10 gallons of gas. And my friend Roman, he got a bottle of rum, and we got two cans of food. And we just decided to have a cool trip on the yacht. And I started saying, oh, I'll go as the open ocean. Let's sail to Poland, I told him.
<v Alex Zharov>我们三个决定去买差不多10加仑汽油。我的朋友罗曼弄了一瓶朗姆酒，我们拿了两罐食物。我们只是决定在游艇上进行一次酷炫的旅行。我开始说，哦，我要去公海。我告诉他，咱们扬帆去波兰吧。

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:15.320
<v Brett Martin>Roman had a slightly less ambitious agenda.
<v Brett Martin>罗曼的计划没有那么雄心勃勃。

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:25.310
<v Roman Gadzhilov>The plan was just to go to the bridge-- under the Rockaway bridge-- then turn around, and then come back. It should have taken about 40 minutes. Yeah.
<v Roman Gadzhilov>计划只是去桥那儿——洛卡威大桥（Rockaway bridge）下面——然后掉头，接着回来。这本该只需要大约40分钟。对。

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:43.880
<v Brett Martin>Things started to go wrong almost immediately. Before they even left the marina, Roman, who'd been making headway through the bottle of rum, fell into the water, and they had to haul him back in. He was clearly in no shape to drive. This is Alex.
<v Brett Martin>事情几乎立刻就开始出岔子了。甚至在他们离开码头之前，一直在一杯接一杯喝着朗姆酒的罗曼就掉进了水里，他们不得不把他捞上来。他显然已经无法驾船了。这是亚历山大在说。

00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:59.750
<v Alex Zharov>He got drunk. And he just was babbling something. Laughing, like he said, don't go there. Don't go there. And he was constantly saying, don't hit the shallows. He was already, like-- he didn't control the situation by that time.
<v Alex Zharov>他喝醉了。他只是在胡言乱语。笑着，就像他说的那样，别去那儿。别去那儿。他不停地说，别撞上浅滩。当时他已经——到那个时候他已经无法掌控局势了。

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:09.940
<v Brett Martin>As a responsible journalist, I should say for the record that Roman does have one objection to Alex's version of events.
<v Brett Martin>作为一名负责任的记者，我必须郑重声明，罗曼对亚历山大的说法确实有一点异议。

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:14.700
<v Roman Gadzhilov>It wasn't the rum, by the way. It was a cognac. I don't know why everybody puts rum. So it was a cognac.
<v Roman Gadzhilov>顺便说一句，那不是朗姆酒。那是干邑。我不知道为什么每个人都说是朗姆酒。所以那是干邑。

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:15.790
<v Brett Martin>You're sure?
<v Brett Martin>你确定吗？

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:16.780
<v Roman Gadzhilov>It was Lautrec.
<v Roman Gadzhilov>那是劳特雷克（Lautrec）。

00:09:16.780 --> 00:09:17.500
<v Brett Martin>OK.
<v Brett Martin>好吧。

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:33.980
<v Roman Gadzhilov>Yes. It was Lautrec cognac. I don't know why-- how come it's become rum. It's probably Alex told it was rum, but it was cognac. Not a little bit. It was a lot. We was out of commission, yeah. I was out of commission.
<v Roman Gadzhilov>是的。那是劳特雷克干邑。我不知道为什么——怎么就变成朗姆酒了。可能是亚历山大说那是朗姆酒，但那是干邑。不是一点点。是很多。我们都彻底瘫痪了，对。我彻底瘫痪了。

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:38.850
<v Brett Martin>Alex and Alex had had a few drinks themselves.
<v Brett Martin>亚历山大和亚历山大自己也喝了几杯。

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:44.130
<v Alex Zharov>But we were perfectly sober and everything. We might have had a few drinks, but we were perfectly sober.
<v Alex Zharov>但我们完全是清醒的，什么都好。我们可能喝了几杯，但我们绝对是清醒的。

00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:46.050
<v Brett Martin>But neither of you knows how to drive a boat?
<v Brett Martin>但是你们俩谁都不会开船？

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:55.300
<v Alex Zharov>No. No. But we got ahold of it. It wasn't that hard. So we knew how to drive it, so, like, it didn't seem pretty hard. You turn on the motor, you turn the boat, it turns, cool.
<v Alex Zharov>不。不会。但我们摸清了。这没那么难。所以我们知道怎么开，所以，呃，看起来并不算很难。你发动马达，你转动船，它就转弯，很酷。

00:09:55.300 --> 00:10:15.530
<v Brett Martin>Somehow, they managed to get out of the marina, gun the engine, and take off across the water toward the Marine Park bridge in the distance. Once there, they decided to try to sail to Brighton Beach and headed toward a land mass. But they got confused and turned back to open water. They drank some rum, or maybe cognac. One way or another, they drank a lot of it.
<v Brett Martin>不知怎的，他们设法驶出了码头，猛踩油门，朝着远处的马林帕克大桥（Marine Park bridge）方向驶过水面。到了那里后，他们决定尝试开船去布莱顿海滩，并朝着一块陆地驶去。但他们迷失了方向，又折返回开阔水域。他们喝了一些朗姆酒，或者也许是干邑。不管怎样，他们喝了很多。

00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:44.050
<v Brett Martin>At one point, they almost crashed into a small island. Gas was running low. But they figured that if worst came to worst, they could always put up the sails and still make it home. Then they got caught in a strong current that turned the boat in circles. The perfect time, you would think, to begin to panic. Or-- if you're the kind of person who forgets trouble the moment you're out of it, or even while you're in it-- the perfect time to shoot off all the boat's flares into the water. Just for fun.
<v Brett Martin>有一次，他们差点撞上一座小岛。汽油快用完了。但他们琢磨着，如果最坏的情况发生，他们随时可以升起帆，还是能安全回家。然后他们被一股强劲的潮流卷住，把船转得团团转。你可能会觉得，这是开始恐慌的绝佳时机。或者——如果你是那种一旦脱离险境就会把麻烦忘得精光、甚至身处险境时也一样的人——这就是把船上所有的信号弹全部射入水中的绝佳时机。纯粹为了好玩。

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:54.850
<v Brett Martin>Finally, the series of mistakes reached a critical mass. They had no cell phone. Roman's had died when he fell in the water. No flares, no captain, and almost no gas. Even Alex had to admit they were in trouble.
<v Brett Martin>最后，这一系列错误达到了临界点。他们没有手机。罗曼的手机在落水时已经坏了。没有信号弹，没有船长，而且几乎没有汽油。就连亚历山大也不得不承认他们遇到麻烦了。

00:10:54.850 --> 00:11:09.130
<v Alex Zharov>We didn't know where we were. And then we realized we weren't going to make it anywhere. And we were like, in the morning, we'll figure out what to do. So we went to sleep.
<v Alex Zharov>我们不知道我们在哪儿。然后我们意识到我们哪儿也去不了。我们当时想，到了早上，我们再想办法看怎么办。于是我们睡觉去了。

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:30.190
<v Brett Martin>It was a glorious spring morning on Jamaica Bay-- sun glinting off the water, gulls calling overhead-- as our young pleasure cruisers slumbered. The light filtering into the boat's cabin woke Roman and Alex Glubochansky first, and they came up on deck. What they saw was not good.
<v Brett Martin>牙买加湾迎来了灿烂的春日清晨——阳光在水面上闪烁，海鸥在头顶鸣叫——我们年轻的度假水手们还在酣睡中。渗入船舱的光线首先唤醒了罗曼和亚历山大·格鲁博尚斯基，他们来到甲板上。他们看到的景象可不妙。

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:47.620
<v Brett Martin>After drifting through the night, the boat had come to rest in the shallows of a small bay alongside an uninhabited landmass. Stretching out behind them, they could see a long furrow where the tide had dragged them deep into thick mud. And as they stood there, blinking and wondering how this might have happened, the wind carried them another 10 feet inland.
<v Brett Martin>在漂流了一夜之后，这艘船搁浅在一个无人居住的陆地旁的一个小海湾浅滩上。在他们身后延伸开去的是一道长长的沟渠，那是退潮时将他们拖入厚厚淤泥深处时留下的痕迹。正当他们站在那里，眨着眼睛，纳闷这一切是怎么发生的时候，一阵风把他们又往内陆吹了10英尺。

00:11:47.620 --> 00:12:14.280
<v Brett Martin>They could see the skyline of Manhattan on the horizon, the runways of JFK airport a little closer, and signs of civilization in every direction. They could even see boats passing by in the distance, but these were too far away to take any notice. It was obvious that they were-- in a word-- shipwrecked.
<v Brett Martin>他们可以看到地平线上曼哈顿的天际线，稍微近一点的是肯尼迪机场（JFK airport）的跑道，四面八方都能看到文明社会的迹象。他们甚至能看到远处有船只驶过，但那些船太远了，根本注意不到他们。显而易见，用一个词来形容——他们遇难了。

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:43.030
<v Brett Martin>The hungover sailors sat down to decide what to do. Roman and Glubochansky were in favor of waiting to be rescued, or for the tide to rise and pull them out again. Meanwhile, Alex was formulating his own plan. Beyond the island they were closest to lay another landmass, which Alex was sure led somewhere. His idea was to swim to it, walk to civilization, catch a bus somewhere, and bring back help for his friends-- who, as Alex remembers it-- thought the plan was, frankly, idiotic.
<v Brett Martin>宿醉的水手们坐下来决定该怎么办。罗曼和格鲁博尚斯基主张等待救援，或者等潮水上涨把他们浮起来再拉出去。与此同时，亚历山大正在酝酿他自己的计划。在他们最近的这座岛屿之外，还有另一块陆地，亚历山大确信那条路通往某处。他的想法是游到那儿去，走到有文明的地方，随便坐辆公交车，然后带救兵回来帮他的朋友们——在亚历山大记忆中，他的朋友们坦率地认为这个计划简直蠢透了。

00:12:43.030 --> 00:13:12.810
<v Brett Martin>These are islands, said Roman, who, in truth, had actually been out on the Bay before and was in a position to know. But Alex was sure that Roman was wrong. So Alex stripped to his underwear. He put what he thought he might need in a waterproof plastic mayonnaise jar. He brought his MetroCard for the bus he was going to swim to, an expired passport for ID, and his favorite Buddhist medallion for luck. He wrapped his clothes in a cellophane blanket and bid his friends farewell. Roman watched him disappear into the surf.
<v Brett Martin>罗曼说，这些都是岛屿。说实话，罗曼以前曾经去过海湾，对此是有发言权的。但亚历山大坚信罗曼搞错了。于是亚历山大脱得只剩内衣。他把他觉得可能用得上的东西放进了一个防水塑料蛋黄酱罐里。他带上了用来坐公交车的地铁卡（MetroCard）、一张过期的护照作为身份证，还有他最喜欢的祈求好运的佛教奖章。他用塑料薄膜毯把衣服裹起来，向他的朋友们告别。罗曼看着他消失在海浪中。

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:36.150
<v Roman Gadzhilov>Of course, I tried to stop him. I tried to give him reasonable things, but he get a little bit too much excited. So I decide to give him a challenge in life. What? Should I just knock him down and say, stop it? You know? He wanted to swim. You know, he wanted to swim, and he swam.
<v Roman Gadzhilov>当然，我试图阻止他。我试着跟他说些道理，但他有点太兴奋了。所以我决定在生活中给他一个挑战。什么？难道我该把他打倒在地然后说，住手？你知道吗？他想游过去。你知道的，他想游，他就游了。

00:13:36.150 --> 00:14:02.870
<v Alex Zharov>I swam really violently to get myself warmed up. And by the middle, I got really tired, and it was really cold. I'm like, aw, this is much worse than I thought. And there's birds flying, like picking on me. I'm like, oh these crazy, strange, Far Rockaway birds are going to bite me or something, you know? And I got really lucky, because my legs suddenly hit the bottom.
<v Alex Zharov>我拼命地游来让自己暖和起来。游到中间时，我真的很累，而且真的很冷。我心想，天哪，这比我想象的要糟糕得多。还有鸟在飞，就像在啄我一样。我想，哦，这些疯狂、奇怪的法罗卡威（Far Rockaway）鸟要来咬我什么的，你知道吗？我真是走运，因为我的双脚突然触到了海底。

00:14:02.870 --> 00:14:24.390
<v Alex Zharov>And I'm like-- and I was so happy when I came out of there. I was so cold, but I was happy. OK. And I was definitely sure there was civilization, because tall buildings were right behind the trees. They were like-- and the bridge was right over there. And I'm like, oh, finally. And I was even singing a song walking. And the birds were screaming something to me. And I'm like, yeah, you know, I made it.
<v Alex Zharov>我想——我从那儿出来的时候高兴极了。虽然冷得要命，但我很高兴。好吧。而且我百分之百确信这里有人烟，因为树林后面就是高楼大厦。它们就在那儿——桥就在那边。我想，哦，总算到了。我甚至边走边唱着歌。树上的鸟儿好像也在冲我尖叫着什么。我想，太好了，你知道的，我成功了。

00:14:24.390 --> 00:14:29.140
<v Brett Martin>I'm still not sure I understand why you left your friends, though.
<v Brett Martin>不过我还是不太明白，你为什么要离开你的朋友们？

00:14:29.140 --> 00:14:43.540
<v Alex Zharov>Because I thought we were going to be stuck there for a really long time. Maybe for the whole day. The only thing I could do is just try to get to civilization. And especially-- these islands, they were pressuring me to go there, you know? They were so close.
<v Alex Zharov>因为我觉得我们会在那儿被困好久。也许一整天。我唯一能做的就是设法走到有人烟的地方。特别是——这些岛屿一直在吸引我去那里，你知道吗？它们离得太近了。

00:14:43.540 --> 00:14:59.990
<v Alex Zharov>And I'm like-- and I got really bored, you know. I wake up in the morning, and I don't want to stay in one spot on the yacht and, like, think about how are we going to get saved, you know? I really want to do something. And I'm like, OK. I'm going to have this little adventure. I'm going to go out and try to make it somewhere. And I did.
<v Alex Zharov>而且我觉得——我真的觉得很无聊，你知道的。我早上醒来，不想一直待在游艇上的某个地方，然后去想我们要怎么获救，你知道吗？我真的很想做点什么。于是我想，好吧。我要展开这场小小的冒险。我要出去，设法去到某个地方。我做到了。

00:14:59.990 --> 00:15:17.700
<v Brett Martin>Except he didn't. Soon, he realized that he was, indeed, on another island, with no way off except to swim back through the freezing water to rejoin his friends. And he wasn't about to do that. He was alone. So Alex set about doing all the things a good castaway should do.
<v Brett Martin>可惜他并没有。很快，他意识到自己确实是在另一座岛上，除了游过冰冷的水回到朋友身边外，根本没有别的出路。而他可不想那么做。他孤身一人。于是亚历克斯开始着手做所有优秀的落难者该做的事。

00:15:17.700 --> 00:15:46.270
<v Brett Martin>He wrote a giant help in the sand for the benefit of the planes landing at JFK. He circumnavigated the island looking for supplies. He found a stick and a piece of red cloth and made a flag to signal passing ships. Then he found several big pieces of Styrofoam and some wood and spent an hour or two fashioning a raft, but it collapsed when he sat down on it. Undeterred, he went back to searching for something that would be his ticket off the island. And then he found it. It was the hollowed out carcass of a jet ski-- or as he calls it, a scooter.
<v Brett Martin>他在沙滩上写下了巨大的求救信号“HELP”，好让在肯尼迪机场降落的飞机看到。他环岛一周寻找补给。他找到了一根棍子和一块红布，做了一面旗帜来给过往的船只发信号。接着，他找到了几大块泡沫塑料和一些木头，花了一两个小时拼凑成了一个木筏，但他刚一坐上去它就散架了。他毫不气馁，继续寻找能带他离开这座岛的东西。然后，他找到了。那是一辆被掏空了内胆的水上摩托——或者按他的话说，是一辆滑板车。

00:15:46.270 --> 00:16:11.730
<v Alex Zharov>I knew-- I 100% knew that it was going to float, although it was pretty badly dug into the sand. And as I was digging out the scooter, something really bad happened. Like there was pieces of glass under it, and I didn't see. I was just digging and digging. And I didn't have any shovel or anything. And I cut my finger really bad. I started getting huge amounts of blood was coming out. And I had this white T-shirt that was eventually all in blood.
<v Alex Zharov>我知道——我百分之百确信它会漂浮，尽管它深深地陷在沙子里。就在我挖这辆滑板车的时候，一件很糟糕的事发生了。它下面有些碎玻璃我没看见。我只是不停地挖啊挖。我没有任何铲子之类的工具。结果我的手指划破得很严重。开始涌出大量的血。我当时穿着一件白T恤，最后全沾满了血。

00:16:11.730 --> 00:16:23.770
<v Brett Martin>Now, there was really no way off the island, even by swimming-- because, well, you know, sharks. It was a galling situation. And it was made even more maddening because the city was right there.
<v Brett Martin>现在，真的没有任何办法离开这个岛了，连游泳都不行——因为，嗯，你知道的，有鲨鱼。这真是一个令人恼火的处境。更让人发疯的是，这座城市明明就在眼前。

00:16:23.770 --> 00:17:02.580
<v Alex Zharov>I was thinking, how the hell did I get myself into this situation? I never believed that something like this could happen in, like, New York City, you know? Like, and it's such a huge city that you could see skyscrapers like 10 miles away. And on the other side, you can die looking at them, you know? And also, I got a little mad at the city of New York. Like, I could understand if they had just one payphone there. Or at least, I don't know, like a button to press to know that you're there, you know?
<v Alex Zharov>我当时在想，我到底是怎么把自己搞到这种境地的？我从来不相信在纽约市这种地方会发生这种事，你知道吗？而且这是一座如此巨大的城市，你甚至能看到10英里外的摩天大楼。而在另一边，你却会看着它们死去，你知道吗？而且，我对纽约市还有点生气。如果他们那里只有一个公用电话，我还能理解。或者至少，我不知道，比如按一个按钮就能让人知道你在这里，你知道吗？

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:18.490
<v Alex Zharov>By probably 6 o'clock in the evening, it was getting a little dark. All my excitement has fled away, and I got very cold. So I was like shaking, you know? Shivering, and no help at all. So I'm like, wow, this is going to get really bad.
<v Alex Zharov>大概到傍晚6点左右，天色开始暗了下来。我所有的兴奋都烟消云散了，而且我感到非常寒冷。所以我在发抖，你知道吗？瑟瑟发抖，而且完全得不到任何帮助。所以我想，哇，这下情况可真要变得糟糕透顶了。

00:17:18.490 --> 00:17:21.370
<v Brett Martin>Were you hungry at this point also?
<v Brett Martin>这时候你也饿了吗？

00:17:21.370 --> 00:17:36.160
<v Alex Zharov>I was very hungry, and I was very thirsty. And I found limes. I tried to open them up, but they tasted so nasty. I couldn't-- I didn't even think about eating them. There was no source of food other than the ducks.
<v Alex Zharov>我当时又饿又渴。我找到了酸橙。我试着把它们掰开，但味道太难吃了。我根本——我甚至没考虑过吃它们。除了鸭子，这里没有任何食物来源。

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:39.080
<v Brett Martin>Ah, yes the ducks. You'll want to hear about the ducks.
<v Brett Martin>啊，对，鸭子。你们肯定想听听关于鸭子的事。

00:17:39.080 --> 00:17:59.350
<v Alex Zharov>If I wasn't going to get rescued in the next hour or two, I had a plan to kill a bunch of ducks to get some warm blood to warm myself, you know? So to drink some blood and to cut them open and use them, like to warm myself. I had this strange idea about use them as slippers.
<v Alex Zharov>如果我在接下来的一两个小时内还获救不了，我甚至盘算着杀掉一堆鸭子，取点热血来暖和暖和自己，你知道吗？喝点血，然后把它们剖开并利用它们，用来取暖。我当时脑子里有个奇怪的想法，想把它们当拖鞋穿。

00:17:59.350 --> 00:18:21.450
<v Alex Zharov>I even had-- after that, I even had this psychedelic idea of floating on the ducks. Making a raft out of the ducks. Imagine a man with strings attached to the ducks, floating on the water, so it's like this duck rider, you know?
<v Alex Zharov>在那之后，我甚至还产生了一个迷幻的想法，那就是浮在鸭子上。用鸭子做一个木筏。想象一下，一个男人身上绑着绳子系在鸭子上，漂浮在水面上，就像一个骑鸭骑士一样，你知道吗？

00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:29.280
<v Alex Zharov>Totally normal for a Russian hiker to go and pick up a duck-- not just to kill it, but to eat it. Like--
<v Alex Zharov>对于一个俄罗斯徒步旅行者来说，去抓一只鸭子——不仅要杀了它，还要吃掉它——这完全是很正常的。就像——

00:18:29.280 --> 00:18:35.370
<v Brett Martin>I'm still-- I can't-- I don't-- like you could just go over and pick up the duck? Like how did you catch the duck?
<v Brett Martin>我还是——我不能——我搞不懂——就像你可以直接走过去抓起那只鸭子一样？你是怎么抓到鸭子的？

00:18:35.370 --> 00:19:04.370
<v Alex Zharov>Oh you just go after it with a stick. I mean, you're a human being. You got more brains than a duck. You can catch it. But I wasn't really thinking about doing it. I wasn't, like, fantasizing about killing ducks or anything like that. I was just thinking that if it comes to that, I'll have to get some blood to drink, you know?
<v Alex Zharov>哦，你拿着根棍子追着它就行了。我的意思是，你是个活生生的人。你的脑子比鸭子聪明。你抓得到的。但我其实没真打算这么做。我并没有幻想过杀鸭子之类的事情。我只是在想，如果真到了那一步，我得弄点血来喝，你知道吗？

00:19:04.370 --> 00:19:27.310
<v Alex Zharov>I know it sounds very violent, but I was fighting for my life, you know? Like, people might laugh when they hear about being trapped on an island that's so close to civilization. And the sharks, and the ducks-- I knew it was a funny situation. But I really got the feeling of what it was like being on a desert island. I felt like Robinson Crusoe, you know?
<v Alex Zharov>我知道这听起来很暴力，但我当时是在为生存而战，你知道吗？人们听到有人被困在离文明如此之近的岛上时可能会嘲笑。还有鲨鱼和鸭子——我知道这情况挺滑稽的。但我真的体验到了身处荒岛是什么感觉。我感觉自己就像鲁滨逊一样，你知道吗？

00:19:27.310 --> 00:19:48.320
<v Alex Zharov>I knew what it was like to be by yourself, away from civilization, with no help, and you're facing this huge problem. And the only person that's near you is you and the ghost of your death close by, you know? So I could smell the-- smell my death in the air.
<v Alex Zharov>我懂得了独自一人远离文明、求助无门，去面对这个巨大难题是什么滋味。离你最近的人只有你自己，还有你身边死神的阴影，你知道吗？所以我能闻到——闻到空气中我自己的死亡气息。

00:19:48.320 --> 00:20:25.030
<v Brett Martin>It turns out that the island where Alex was stranded is called Ruffle Bar, and it lies only a 20-minute boat ride away from the coast of Brooklyn. Far from being traumatized or ashamed of his exploits, Alex wanted nothing more than to go back out there. And from the advantage of my overpriced, under-sized apartment, I wanted to see a place where you could be totally alone in the wilderness, smelling your own death in the air, while in at least theoretical commuting distance to Midtown Manhattan. So we hired a boat to take us to Ruffle Bar.
<v Brett Martin>事实证明，亚历克斯搁浅的那座岛名叫拉弗尔巴（Ruffle Bar），乘船从布鲁克林海岸出发只需20分钟。亚历克斯不仅没有因这次经历而受到心理创伤或感到羞耻，反而一心只想再回那里去。坐在我那间价格虚高、空间狭小的公寓里，我也很想去看看那个地方——在那里你可以完全独自一人身处荒野，呼吸着自己死亡的气息，同时在理论上距离曼哈顿中城也算通勤距离之内。于是我们租了一艘船，带我们前往拉弗尔巴。

00:20:25.030 --> 00:20:49.790
<v Brett Martin>In truth, I wasn't as completely surprised as some might be to learn that such a place exists. I grew up near the islands of Jamaica Bay in a neighborhood called Canarsie. And when I was little, my friends and I would cut through the empty lots near my house to explore the mix of trash and nature on the shoreline. It was a place totally apart from the rest of my mostly urban childhood. A secret place that my friends who lived even 10 or 15 blocks away were unaware existed.
<v Brett Martin>说实话，对于这样一个地方的存在，我并没有像某些人那样感到全然的惊讶。我是在牙买加湾的群岛附近一个叫卡纳西（Canarsie）的街区长大的。我小的时候，我和朋友们会穿过我家附近的空地，去探索海岸线上垃圾与自然交织的景象。那是一个完全脱离了我大部分都市童年生活的世界。一个连只住在十几个街区之外的朋友都不知道它存在的秘密地方。

00:20:49.790 --> 00:21:15.930
<v Brett Martin>But then the smaller islands around New York have always occupied a weird place on the edge of the city. Home to all sorts of enterprise that the citizenry either doesn't know about or prefers not to see. Sanitariums and prisons, potter's fields, and grand failed schemes. Ruffle Bar itself had been the site of several of the latter. Since the Civil War, it had housed a ferry stop, a resort hotel, and even a short-lived dune community of some 40 buildings. We stop in front of a concrete foundation.
<v Brett Martin>但话说回来，纽约周围的那些小岛一直在城市边缘占据着一个奇特的位置。那里是市民们要么不了解、要么选择视而不见的各种机构的所在地。疗养院、监狱、无名墓地，以及那些宏大却破产的计划。拉弗尔巴本身就曾是后者的好几个项目的所在地。自南北战争以来，这里曾设有渡轮站、度假酒店，甚至还有一个存在时间短暂、由大约40栋建筑组成沙丘社区。我们在一个混凝土基座前停了下来。

00:21:15.930 --> 00:21:17.760
<v Brett Martin>A building of some kind was here.
<v Brett Martin>这里曾经有过某种建筑。

00:21:17.760 --> 00:21:32.780
<v Alex Zharov>Oh, look this is a cool thing. This is one of the World War II things that's here. Like, you open them up, and you can go inside. There's like a room in there. It might be something like a bunker or something. You see the rope here? And the rope is really old. Let me take a picture of this.
<v Alex Zharov>哦，看，这个挺酷的。这是这里的二战遗留设施之一。你可以把它们打开，然后走进去。里面好像有个房间。可能是地堡之类的东西。你看到这根绳子了吗？这绳子真的很旧。让我拍张照。

00:21:32.780 --> 00:21:46.340
<v Brett Martin>There are no buildings left here. The island is returned to a deeply wild state. There's a wall of dense brush and a few trees around which sinister gulls are circling. We pass a flock of ducks who take one look at Alex and wisely move away.
<v Brett Martin>这里已经没有任何建筑物了。这座岛屿已经恢复了极度野生的状态。这里有一堵密集的灌木墙和几棵树，一些不祥的海鸥在周围盘旋。我们路过一群鸭子，它们看了亚历克斯一眼，明智地走开了。

00:21:46.340 --> 00:21:59.510
<v Alex Zharov>Well I don't think it's-- I am really thinking about where the heck is this scooter, because it seems like it should be, like, as we turn, there should be more shoreline here. Maybe the--
<v Alex Zharov>嗯，我觉得这——我其实在想这辆滑板车到底在哪儿，因为看起来应该是这样的，当我们拐弯时，这里应该还有更多海岸线。也许——

00:21:59.510 --> 00:22:01.770
<v Brett Martin>Is this it?
<v Brett Martin>是这里吗？

00:22:01.770 --> 00:22:10.630
<v Alex Zharov>Yup, that's exactly it. Oh, wow. This is the scooter I tried to dig out. Let me show you. Maybe you'll see the glass and stuff. That's awesome.
<v Alex Zharov>对，就是这里。哦，哇。这就是我当年试图挖出来的那个滑板车。我给你看看。也许你能看到那些玻璃之类的东西。太棒了。

00:22:10.630 --> 00:22:31.250
<v Brett Martin>As we search for Alex's Buddhist medallion that he'd left in the excitement of the helicopter rescue, we walk across a plane of thick, dry grass, matted down like a carpet. Underneath, you can hear shells crunching and mysterious things scurrying around. Still, reminders that we are, in fact, in a major metropolis are always close at hand.
<v Brett Martin>当我们寻找亚历克斯在直升机救援的慌乱中遗落的那枚佛教奖章时，我们走过一片厚重、干燥的草地，草像地毯一样被压扁了。在草皮下面，你能听到贝壳碎裂的声音和神秘生物四处逃窜的声音。不过，时刻提醒着我们其实身处繁华大都市的痕迹依然触手可得。

00:22:31.250 --> 00:22:54.190
<v Brett Martin>For one thing, there's the garbage. Piles of plastic and driftwood, but also shoes, steering wheels, prescription bottles, deflated balloons, a washer dryer, several refrigerators, and, oddly, boats. Three perfectly intact ones, complete with oars. I hesitate to point these out to Alex, though, to be fair, they're probably too heavy for him to have dragged to the water. And then there's this reminder of civilization.
<v Brett Martin>一方面，这里有垃圾。成堆的塑料和流木，还有鞋子、方向盘、处方药瓶、瘪了的气球、洗衣烘干机、好几台冰箱，以及奇怪的是，还有船。三艘完好无损的船，里面还配有船桨。不过我犹豫着要不要把这些指给亚历克斯看，公平地说，这些船可能太重了，他当时根本拉不到水里去。接着，这就是文明的另一个提醒。

00:22:54.190 --> 00:22:55.100
<v Brett Martin>[PHONE RINGING]
<v Brett Martin>[电话铃声]

00:22:55.100 --> 00:23:03.410
<v Alex           Zharov>Hold on. [SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Alex           Zharov>等等。[说俄语]

00:23:03.410 --> 00:23:30.930
<v Brett Martin>He was always close enough to the city that simply having a cell phone would have had him tucked safely into bed within half an hour. Alex was finally rescued after seven hours, thanks to Roman and Glubochansky. Back on the boat, they were having a fine old time. A police helicopter was performing drills nearby. And apparently-- no slouches in the clicheed castaway department themselves-- they had figured out that they could signal it with a mirror. But why rush?
<v Brett Martin>他离城市其实一直都很近，只要有个手机，半小时内就能安稳地躺在床上了。在罗曼（Roman）和格卢博昌斯基（Glubochansky）的帮助下，亚历克斯在经历了七个小时后终于获救了。回到船上，他们过得挺开心的。一架警用直升机正在附近进行演习。显然——他们自己在扮演落难者的老套路上也毫不含糊——他们已经琢磨出可以用一面镜子向直升机发信号。但为什么要急着离开呢？

00:23:30.930 --> 00:23:52.520
<v Roman Gadzhilov>We really enjoyed the time staying there. We was just sitting in the boat and, you know, smoking the last tobacco that we had left. And we make a deal that we're not going to eat each other if we're really going to get hungry. So basically, it was having fun. You know, just a little bit. No hustle, no nothing. You know, very quiet. Nice weather.
<v Roman Gadzhilov>我们在那儿待得很开心。我们只是坐在船上，你知道的，抽着我们剩下的最后一点烟。我们还达成了一个协议，如果我们真的饿得受不了了，绝对不互相吃对方。所以基本上，就是玩得很开心。你知道的，稍微放松一下。没有喧嚣，什么都没有。非常安静。天气很好。

00:23:52.520 --> 00:23:57.190
<v Brett Martin>Oh, so you didn't-- so you were actually holding off signaling the helicopters while you had a nice day?
<v Brett Martin>哦，所以你们没有——所以你们在享受美好时光的同时，其实一直拖着没向直升机发信号？

00:23:57.190 --> 00:24:03.060
<v Roman Gadzhilov>Yeah, of course. It was a nice day.
<v Roman Gadzhilov>对，当然。那天天气很好。

00:24:03.060 --> 00:24:22.650
<v Brett Martin>Still, as it began to get dark and the cigarettes ran out, the friends thought it was probably time to get a move on. The helicopter soon arrived and airlifted them off the boat. It wasn't until they were safely ashore, wrapped in blankets and being fed complimentary cookies, that either of them happened to mention that there had been a third passenger.
<v Brett Martin>不过，随着天色渐暗，香烟也抽完了，朋友们觉得差不多该动身了。直升机很快就到了，将他们从船上空运接走。直到他们安全上了岸，裹着毛毯并吃着免费的曲奇饼干时，他们俩才偶然提到了其实还有第三名乘客。

00:24:22.650 --> 00:24:34.590
<v Brett Martin>When the helicopter came back for Alex, cold, exhaustion, and dehydration had left him in a trance-like, almost wild state. And for him, this island will always be a place where maybe there'd be monsters.
<v Brett Martin>当直升机折返回来接亚历克斯时，寒冷、精疲力竭和脱水已经使他处于一种恍惚的、几乎是野兽般的状态。对汉来说，这座岛将永远是一个可能潜藏着怪物的世界。

00:24:34.590 --> 00:24:48.870
<v Alex Zharov>And I was actually-- when I was here, I was wondering if it's like totally wild place. Are there any animals here other than birds? I was maybe hoping to see some cool animal, like a badger or something. I don't know. I like badgers a lot.
<v Alex Zharov>而且我当时——我在这里的时候，我在想这到底是不是一个完全野生的世界。除了鸟类之外这里还有其他动物吗？我其实有点希望能看到一些酷炫的动物，比如獾什么的。我不知道。我非常喜欢獾。

00:24:48.870 --> 00:24:49.980
<v Brett Martin>Is that right?
<v Brett Martin>是吗？

00:24:49.980 --> 00:25:11.370
<v Alex Zharov>Yeah, it's one of my favorite animals. You know, I like badgers for the same reason probably I like the state of Utah, where I never was. It's like something that has some kind of-- what's it called-- like a secret, or it's hiding, or it's like-- they attract me in the way that they might be hiding something cool from me.
<v Alex Zharov>对，它是我最喜欢的动物之一。你知道的，我喜欢獾的原因，可能和我喜欢犹他州的原因差不多，虽然我从未去过那里。那是一种带有某种——怎么说呢——神秘感的东西，或者它在隐藏自己，或者它像——它们吸引我的地方在于，它们可能在向我隐瞒一些很酷的事情。

00:25:11.370 --> 00:25:29.330
<v Brett Martin>And that's what, after many hours spent with Alex, I find myself liking about him the most-- his insistence on finding mystery and adventure everywhere he looks. It's easy to laugh at that, to write it all off as adolescent stupidity. But what if it's more than that? What if it's also a kind of adolescent magic?
<v Brett Martin>这就是在与亚历克斯相处了许多个小时后，我发现自己最喜欢他的地方——他坚持在所到之处寻找神秘与冒险。嘲笑这一点很容易，把它一笔勾画成青春期的愚蠢也很容易。但如果这不仅于此呢？如果这也是一种青春期的魔法呢？

00:25:29.330 --> 00:25:48.880
<v Alex Zharov>Actually, I'm thinking that this needed to happen, you know? I think if I was a boring person and I would just, like, stay at home all the time, and be, like, a nerd, I would never get into this situation. So I think this happened strictly because I was with the right people at the right time, like, in the right situation.
<v Alex Zharov>其实，我在想，这一切都是命中注定的，你知道吗？我想如果我是一个无趣的人，整天只知道待在家里，像个书呆子，我绝对不会陷入这种境地。所以我认为这件事发生，纯粹是因为我在对的时间、和对的人一起，处在了对的情境中。

00:25:48.880 --> 00:26:03.870
<v Brett Martin>Think about that. Every step of the way, by almost any measure, Alex could not have been more wrong. It takes a special kind of grace to turn that into right time, right place. And how can you help but envy that?
<v Brett Martin>想想看。在每一个环节，几乎用任何标准来衡量，亚历克斯都不可能错得更离谱了。要把这种局面变成“天时地利”，需要一种特殊的天赋。你怎么能不对此感到羡慕呢？

00:26:03.870 --> 00:26:19.370
<v Brett Martin>Who wouldn't rather live in a world where if you believe you should have an adventure, you do? In which each of your mistakes doesn't narrow your life, but expands it? In which the worst thing that could possibly happen is being bored, and you can go to sleep on stormy seas and trust that when you wake up, if you're very lucky, you'll be in Utah?
<v Brett Martin>谁不想生活在这样一个世界里：如果你坚信自己应该去冒险，你就会付诸行动？在这个世界里，你的每一次失误都不会让你的生活变窄，而是让它变得开阔？在这里，可能发生的最糟糕的事情也不过是感到无聊，你可以在风暴汹涌的大海上安然入睡，并坚信当你醒来时，如果你足够幸运，你就会身处犹他州？

00:26:19.370 --> 00:26:35.310
<v Brett Martin>What I'm trying to say is this. Alex does something I never in a million years would have thought possible. He makes me think it might be cool to be a teenager again.
<v Brett Martin>我想说的是这个。亚历克斯做了一件我做梦也想不到的事。他让我觉得，重新当回一个青少年也许是一件很酷的事。

00:26:35.310 --> 00:27:08.230
<v Brett Martin>There's a story that back in the 1830s, a ship carrying $54,000 in Mexican gold was hijacked by pirates outside Jamaica Bay, and that the treasure was buried somewhere near Ruffle Bar. On our way back from the island, I tell Alex this, and he listens with great interest. If he found the treasure, he wants to know, could he keep it? Maybe, I say. If he didn't tell anybody. To which Alex answers-- precisely as I know he will, the only way he possibly can. He says, but what if I told everybody?
<v Brett Martin>有个传说，早在19世纪30年代，一艘载有5万4千美元墨西哥黄金的船在牙买加湾外被海盗劫持，这批宝藏就被埋在拉弗尔巴附近某处。在从岛上回来的路上，我把这件事告诉了亚历克斯，他听得津津有味。如果他找到了这笔宝藏，他想知道，他能据为己有吗？也许吧，我说。只要他不告诉任何人。对此亚历克斯回答说——正如我所预料的那样，他唯一可能的回答。他说，但如果我把所有人都告诉了呢？

00:27:08.230 --> 00:27:19.220
<v Ira Glass>Brett Martin. He's the author of Difficult Men: Behind the Scenes of a Creative Revolution.
<v Ira Glass>布雷特·马丁（Brett Martin）。他是《难搞的男人：创意革命幕后》（Difficult Men: Behind the Scenes of a Creative Revolution）一书的作者。

00:27:19.220 --> 00:27:30.880
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, the thing about Chicago that nobody outside Chicago believes about Chicago but that actually is completely and totally true. That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来的节目中，将讲述一个关于芝加哥的事实：所有芝加哥以外的人都不相信它，但它其实千真万确。稍后播出，节目来自芝加哥公共广播电台（Chicago Public Radio）。

00:27:30.880 --> 00:27:54.030
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme and bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's program, In the Shadow of the City. Stories about things happening out of sight from most of us, but very close to us. We've arrived at Act Two of our program. Act Two, Troubled Bridge Over Water.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《此生此美》（This American Life）。我是伊拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。当然，我们每周的节目都会选择一个主题，并为您带来各种不同类型的故事。今天的节目是：城市的阴影之下。这些故事讲述的是那些发生在我们大多数人视线之外，却又离我们极近的事情。我们来到了节目的第二幕。第二幕，水上难通之桥。

00:27:54.030 --> 00:28:24.490
<v Ira Glass>In 2003, on the edge of a city halfway around the world, in Nanjing, China, a man named Chen Si-- Mr. Chen-- headed out to a bridge away from his wife and daughter. He was there for 10 hours a day. The bridge he chose is this concrete communist monstrosity, four miles long, covered with slogans that celebrate the worker. Four lanes of traffic and thousands of pedestrians on the top deck. Two train tracks on the lower deck, over the Yangtze River into Nanjing, a city of seven million.
<v Ira Glass>2003年，在地球另一端的一座城市边缘——中国南京，一个名叫陈思的男人——陈先生——离开妻子和女儿，前往一座大桥。他每天在那里待上10个小时。他选择的那座大桥是个混凝土的共产主义庞然大物，长达四英里，上面刷满了歌颂工人的标语。上层桥面有四条车道和成千上万的行人。下层桥面有两条铁轨，横跨长江通往南京这座拥有七百万人口的城市。

00:28:24.490 --> 00:28:50.340
<v Ira Glass>Estimates are fuzzy, but the best guess is that one person per week commits suicide off this bridge. Quick heads up, by the way, that this story is going to discuss suicide. Mr. Chen decided he wanted to try to keep them from jumping. And he started to-- singlehandedly, at first, then with an occasional volunteer. The blog that he keeps about this is the most sober, taciturn, non-boastful account of saving lives imaginable.
<v Ira Glass>估算数据并不精确，但最好的猜测是，每周都有一个人从这座大桥上跳桥自杀。顺便提醒一下，这个故事将会讨论自杀。陈先生决定试着阻止他们跳桥。一开始他单枪匹马，后来偶尔会有志愿者加入。他关于这事的博客是他所能想象到的关于拯救生命的最冷静、寡言、毫不夸耀的记录。

00:28:50.340 --> 00:29:08.080
<v Ira Glass>Occasionally, Mr. Chen will insert his feelings. "Beware heavy thoughts," he declares to himself during one entry. "How I wish that he would soon be free of this shadow," he says about an old man he saved in another. But mostly it's just the facts. Here's a translation from the Chinese.
<v Ira Glass>偶尔，陈先生会融入自己的情感。“谨防沉重的思绪，”他在其中一篇日记中对自己宣告。“多么希望他能早日摆脱这道阴影，”他在另一篇日记中谈到自己救下的一位老人时说。但绝大多数时候都只是事实。以下是从中文翻译过来的内容。

00:29:08.080 --> 00:29:35.620
<v Ira Glass>"On July 25 at 10:30 in the morning, I discovered a woman lying on the bridge railing, on her belly, weeping. I went to her. She wiped her eyes. She said she was just playing, and walked toward the center of the bridge. I went with her, and she-- very ordinarily-- dialed her cell phone. When I returned at 1:10, I discovered that she had already climbed up on the bridge railing. I restrained her and forced her onto a moped. She is from Nanjing's Jianye district.
<v Ira Glass>“7月25日上午10点30分，我发现一名女子趴在桥栏杆上哭泣。我走向她。她擦了擦眼泪。她说她只是在玩耍，然后向桥中心走去。我和她一起走，她很平常地拨打了手机。当我1点10分返回时，我发现她已经爬上了桥栏杆。我制服了她，并强行把她弄上一辆助力车。她是南京建邺区人。

00:29:35.620 --> 00:29:50.870
<v Ira Glass>"Her last name is Zhao, and today she's 45 years old. Because her husband, surnamed Li and 51 years old, is violent towards her and mistreats her, she thought killing herself would be better. However, she is silent when she thinks of her 15-year-old son.
<v Ira Glass>“她姓赵，今年45岁。由于51岁的丈夫姓李，对她施暴并虐待她，她觉得自杀会更好。然而，当她想到15岁的儿子时，她沉默了。

00:29:50.870 --> 00:30:18.890
<v Ira Glass>"March 21, 2010. Yesterday at 3:05 PM, I saved a young man in the middle of the bridge. He had drunk a lot of alcohol and was planning to jump over the bridge railing. I at once restrained him and dragged him to safety. As we spoke, I learned the situation was actually quite funny. He was thinking about jumping because last year, his wife promised to start returning to him 200 Yuan of his monthly 1,400 Yuan salary to spend as he pleased. But she had not honored her promise.
<v Ira Glass>“2010年3月21日。昨天下午3点05分，我在桥中央救了一个年轻人。他喝了很多酒，正打算翻过桥栏杆。我当即制服了他，把他拉到安全地带。交谈中，我得知情况其实挺搞笑的。他之所以想跳桥，是因为去年他的妻子答应每月从他1400元的工资里拿出200元给他自由支配。但她没有兑现承诺。

00:30:18.890 --> 00:30:47.310
<v Ira Glass>"Yesterday afternoon, he started drinking with his friends. And the more he drank, the angrier he got. He believed that killing himself would make her realize that not one cent had come to him. He then said another funny thing. His mother's colleague said that the bridge is haunted and could take one's soul. I said, haha, it is haunted by drunk ghosts, and I took him home. This was the calmest, simplest rescue I've made in recent years."
<v Ira Glass>“昨天下午，他开始和朋友喝酒。越喝越气。他相信自杀能让她意识到自己一分钱也没拿到。接着他又说了件搞笑的事。他母亲的同事说这座大桥闹鬼，能勾走人的灵魂。我说，哈哈，这里闹的是酒鬼的鬼，然后我把他送回家了。这是我近年来最平静、最简单的一次救援。”

00:30:47.310 --> 00:30:59.090
<v Ira Glass>Many of Mr. Chen's entries are about the people that he does not save. "February 15, 5:30 in the morning, a middle aged man jumped to his death. It is reported at this time that he was holding a photograph of his family.
<v Ira Glass>陈先生的许多日记都是关于那些他未能救下的人。“2月15日清晨5点30分，一名中年男子跳桥身亡。据报道，当时他手里紧紧握着一张全家福照片。

00:30:59.090 --> 00:31:27.420
<v Ira Glass>"August 10, 2008, Saturday afternoon at 1:40 PM, a young woman 300 meters from the south end of the bridge climbed under the bridge railing. I immediately started my moped, but because they accelerated too quickly, the moped leaked oil and ignited. I had to run to her. But when I was 200 meters away, she jumped into the Yangtze. Her silhouette was visible in the water at a spot 50 meters away, and I could still hear her yelling for help until a large wave obscured her from view."
<v Ira Glass>“2008年8月10日，星期六下午1点40分，在桥南端300米处，一名年轻女子爬到了桥栏杆下面。我立刻启动了助力车，但由于油门加得太快，助力车漏油并着火了。我不得不跑向她。但当我离她还有200米时，她跳进了长江。她的身影在50米外的水面上隐约可见，我甚至还能听到她呼救的声音，直到一个大浪将她的身影彻底淹没。”

00:31:27.420 --> 00:31:46.460
<v Ira Glass>At the end of each year, Mr. Chen does an inventory of how things are going on the bridge. This one is from the end of 2009. He wrote that since he began back in 2003, he saved at that point 174 people from committing suicide, counseled another 5,150 on the bridge, and 16,000 on the phone.
<v Ira Glass>每年年底，陈先生都会盘点一下桥上的情况。这一份是2009年底的记录。他写道，自2003年开始以来，当时他已经从自杀边缘救下了174人，在桥上劝导了另外5150人，并通过电话劝导了16000人。

00:31:46.460 --> 00:32:03.070
<v Ira Glass>51,000 people had texted him. Total days volunteering to that point-- 646.
<v Ira Glass>有51000人给他发过短信。截至当时的总志愿服务天数——646天。

00:32:03.070 --> 00:32:20.350
<v Ira Glass>Mike Paterniti wrote a magazine article about Mr. Chen. He first heard about him years ago from news reports. He read a bit of his blog in Google translate. He felt like he had to meet this man, who, on his own, had decided to rescue so many people, and flew to China.
<v Ira Glass>迈克·帕特尼蒂（Mike Paterniti）写过一篇关于陈先生的杂志文章。他几年前从新闻报道中首次得知这个人。他用谷歌翻译读了一点陈先生的博客。他觉得自己必须见见这个独自决定拯救如此多生命的人，于是飞往中国。

00:32:20.350 --> 00:32:44.900
<v Mike Paterniti>I thought maybe-- maybe I'll see him in action. Maybe I'll get to see him save somebody. Just as back story, I mean, I actually came-- I had come from Cambodia. So I was covering these genocide trials, so I wasn't-- I didn't have the most optimistic feelings about humanity. And I thought I was going to find something there.
<v Mike Paterniti>我想也许——也许我会看到他在行动。也许我能看到他救下某个人。作为背景，我的意思是，我其实是——我刚从柬埔寨来。当时我在报道那里的种族灭绝审判，所以我并不是——我对人性并没有最乐观的感受。我以为我会在那里找到些什么。

00:32:44.900 --> 00:32:47.740
<v Ira Glass>You thought you were going to meet like, a hopeful figure?
<v Ira Glass>你以为你会遇到一个充满希望的人物？

00:32:47.740 --> 00:33:14.480
<v Mike Paterniti>Yeah, I mean hope, perseverance, generosity. But as soon as I got on the bridge, I realized that all those notions were completely absurd. I mean I got instantly depressed. First of all, there's this four-mile long bridge, and this one man out there sort of trying to pick out who was going to jump. It just seemed, from a distance, like insurmountable odds to actually maybe pull somebody off the bridge.
<v Mike Paterniti>是的，我是说希望、毅力、慷慨。但一上桥，我意识到所有这些想法都完全荒谬。我的意思是，我瞬间就陷入了沮丧。首先，这是一座四英里长的桥，而这个人孤零零地在那里，试图挑出谁要跳桥。从远处看，要真正把某个人从桥上拉下来，似乎有着无法克服的困难。

00:33:14.480 --> 00:33:29.880
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, you wrote in the article at one point, you said, first of all, there's the cars, and there's the trains, and the bridge is shaking, and then there's just like a sea of people-- thousands of people in the rain with umbrellas going back and forth on the bridge. And he's just one guy kind of walking up and down.
<v Ira Glass>是的，你在文章中一度写道，你说，首先，有汽车，有火车，桥在摇晃，然后就像是一片人海——成千上万的人在雨中撑着伞在桥上走来走去。而他只是一个独自走来走去的家伙。

00:33:29.880 --> 00:33:49.340
<v Mike Paterniti>And he has this little moped, and does a little cruise on the bridge every once in a while. But even that is a somewhat comical sight to behold. You know, he's on this little broken-down moped, putt-putting through the crowd with his big pair of binoculars around his neck.
<v Mike Paterniti>他有一辆小助力车，每隔一段时间就在桥上巡视一番。但即使是这一点，看起来也多少有点滑稽。你知道的，他骑着这辆破破烂烂的小助力车，脖子上挂着一副大望远镜，在人群中突突地穿行。

00:33:49.340 --> 00:34:04.550
<v Mike Paterniti>You know, I sort of thought, maybe this isn't even real. Like, maybe this blog is a complete figment of his imagination, or a fiction that he constructs, you know, once a week. And I just don't see how this guy can save anybody out here.
<v Mike Paterniti>你知道，我甚至曾想，这也许根本不是真的。比如，这个博客也许完全是他想象出来的虚构产物，或者他每周编造一次的故事。我实在不明白这家伙怎么能在这里救下任何人。

00:34:04.550 --> 00:34:10.430
<v Ira Glass>And you write in your article, he will he won't really talk to you when you're there on the bridge.
<v Ira Glass>而你在文章中写道，当你在桥上时，他其实并不怎么跟你说话。

00:34:10.430 --> 00:34:17.780
<v Mike Paterniti>Yeah. He is really grumpy and unwilling to acknowledge me.
<v Mike Paterniti>是的。他真的很古怪，不愿意搭理我。

00:34:17.780 --> 00:34:20.800
<v Ira Glass>And so give me a typical exchange between the two of you on the bridge.
<v Ira Glass>那么，请描述一下你们两个人在桥上的典型对话。

00:34:20.800 --> 00:34:51.780
<v Mike Paterniti>I think I did ask, like, why are you standing here as opposed to any other spot on this four-mile-long bridge? And he turned and lifted his binoculars and focused out toward the river, and then brought his binoculars down, turned the other way, put his binoculars up, and focused in the other direction on the crowd.
<v Mike Paterniti>我想我确实问过，比如，你为什么站在这里，而不是在这座四英里长的大桥的其他地方？他转过身，举起望远镜朝江面聚焦，然后放下望远镜，转向另一个方向，举起望远镜，朝人群的另一个方向聚焦。

00:34:51.780 --> 00:34:54.920
<v Ira Glass>But that's it? He doesn't even respond.
<v Ira Glass>但就这样？他连回应都没有。

00:34:54.920 --> 00:35:13.510
<v Mike Paterniti>No. It wasn't like-- it was like I wasn't even there. It was like I was some ghost. And I sort of went through some of this. And then I said, maybe is there a better time for us to talk? And he said to the translator, you know, I can talk to you at lunch.
<v Mike Paterniti>没有。感觉不像是——就好像我根本不在那里。感觉我像是个幽灵。我经历了一番这样的对待。然后我说，我们谈话是不是挑个更好的时间比较好？他对翻译说，你知道的，我可以在午饭时跟你谈。

00:35:13.510 --> 00:35:17.150
<v Ira Glass>So you go to lunch with him, and what happens there?
<v Ira Glass>所以你跟他一起去吃午饭了，那里发生了什么？

00:35:17.150 --> 00:35:32.240
<v Mike Paterniti>Well, so we were in a little what they call family restaurant near the bridge. And there are no families present. I mean, it's just workers. And they're pretty hard-drinking-- in this case, grain alcohol and beer.
<v Mike Paterniti>嗯，我们在桥附近一个小型的所谓家庭餐馆里。那里没有什么家庭。我的意思是，全都是工人。而且他们很能喝酒——在这种情况下，喝的是白酒和啤酒。

00:35:32.240 --> 00:35:33.160
<v Ira Glass>Mhm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯哼。

00:35:33.160 --> 00:36:04.120
<v Mike Paterniti>And so we sit down at the table. And Mr. Chen has invited a man to join us whose name is Mr. Xi. And then we are served some food. And Mr. Chen and Mr. Xi start really drinking a lot of grain alcohol. And I started to, sort of, drink with them because it was the convivial thing to do. And then I just realized, I am going to pass out if I try to stay with these guys.
<v Mike Paterniti>于是我们在桌旁坐下。陈先生邀请了一位名叫习先生的男士加入我们。然后我们上了一些菜。陈先生和习先生开始猛灌白酒。我也开始陪着他们喝一点，因为入乡随俗嘛。然后我突然意识到，如果我继续跟这几个家伙喝下去，我绝对会当场昏过去。

00:36:04.120 --> 00:36:26.820
<v Mike Paterniti>I mean, like, I'm literally-- I was-- my head was spinning and I was-- the whole room was revolving. I just was like-- and he was very disappointed. And so he sort of said, you know, just we're drinking here, this is what we do at lunch. And drinking loosens the tongue, and so get with the program. And if you can't, then why don't you put on a dress?
<v Mike Paterniti>我的意思是，字面意义上的——我当时——我脑袋晕乎乎的，整个房间都在旋转。我当时就觉得——而他对此感到非常失望。所以他大概说了句，你知道的，我们在这儿喝酒，这就是我们午饭时的作风。喝酒能让人打开话匣子，所以入乡随俗吧。如果你做不到，那干脆穿条裙子算了？

00:36:26.820 --> 00:36:40.320
<v Mike Paterniti>But then he-- you know, at lunch he definitely opened up a little bit more. I mean, he wasn't looking at me when he answered questions. But he was answering them, and he was speaking more expansively about life on the bridge.
<v Mike Paterniti>但随后他——你知道，在午饭期间他确实稍微敞开心扉了。我的意思是，当他回答问题时并没有看着我。但他确实在回答，而且对桥上的生活谈论得更广泛了。

00:36:40.320 --> 00:36:42.620
<v Ira Glass>Did he explain why it is that he does this?
<v Ira Glass>他有没有解释他为什么要这么做？

00:36:42.620 --> 00:37:14.170
<v Mike Paterniti>He said he had read a newspaper article about the bridge, and about people jumping off the bridge. And he himself had grown up in the country outside of Nanjing. So he really related in particular to these people from the villages who came to the bridge to end their lives, and whose lives were hard and full of despair. And he completely understood that.
<v Mike Paterniti>他说他读过一篇关于这座桥以及人们跳桥的新闻报道。他自己就是在南京郊区的农村长大的。所以他特别能对这些从村庄来到桥上结束生命、生活艰难且充满绝望的人感同身受。他完全理解这一点。

00:37:14.170 --> 00:37:20.370
<v Ira Glass>So you go back up to the bridge, and he putters off on his moped. And then--
<v Ira Glass>于是你回到了桥上，他骑着助力车突突地走了。然后——

00:37:20.370 --> 00:37:41.970
<v Mike Paterniti>Yeah, and then he jumped on his moped to go on his rounds. And I didn't have anything to do, but I turned to the translator, Susan, and I said, hey, let's take a little walk out on the bridge. And so we started walking out over the bridge. And we're chatting a little bit.
<v Mike Paterniti>是的，然后他跳上助力车去巡视了。我无事可做，但我转头对翻译苏珊说，嘿，咱们在桥上散散步吧。于是我们开始往桥上走。我们一边走一边闲聊。

00:37:41.970 --> 00:38:25.500
<v Mike Paterniti>And this guy kind of came lurching by. And he-- I didn't pay any attention to him. But this guy is about 20 feet-- 30 feet ahead of us, and he seems to be climbing up on the railing. And at that point, I just yelled, hey! And then I said to Susan, he's going to go over. And I started running for him, and Susan came running. And I had that one little flash of Mr. Chen saying, some of these people will really take you with them if they can. They're that desperate.
<v Mike Paterniti>这时有个家伙踉踉跄跄地走过来。我——我没怎么注意他。但这个家伙在我们前面大约20英尺——30英尺的地方，他似乎在往栏杆上爬。就在那时，我大喊了一声，嘿！然后我对苏珊说，他要跳下去了。我开始朝他跑去，苏珊也跑了过来。我脑子里闪过陈先生说过的话：这些人中有些人在可能的时候真的会把你一起拉下去。他们就是这么绝望。

00:38:25.500 --> 00:38:31.050
<v Mike Paterniti>And I had that little flash, like, this would be a stupid way to die. This would be ridiculous if I--
<v Mike Paterniti>我当时脑子里飞快地闪过一个念头：用这种方式死太蠢了。如果我——

00:38:31.050 --> 00:38:32.780
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:38:32.780 --> 00:39:03.750
<v Mike Paterniti>--go down with this guy. But it didn't come to that because when I got to him, I had my foot on the inside of the, sort of, the concrete buttress. And I tried to flip him back toward me. And he was completely limp. He was like a bag of sawdust. He just flipped right back onto me. And I hadn't even really pulled him that hard.
<v Mike Paterniti>——跟着这家伙一起掉下去，那就太荒谬了。但事情并没有发展到那一步，因为当我冲到他身边时，我的脚踩在了混凝土桥墩的内侧。我试图把他往我这边扳回来。他整个人完全瘫软了。他就像一袋锯末。他直接倒在了我身上。其实我甚至都没怎么用力拉他。

00:39:03.750 --> 00:39:10.550
<v Mike Paterniti>It's hard to explain, but when I think of it, I just get-- I have to say, just I have just goosebumps all over my body right now.
<v Mike Paterniti>这很难解释，但每当我想起这件事，我就会——我不得不说，我现在全身都起鸡皮疙瘩。

00:39:10.550 --> 00:39:16.640
<v Ira      Glass>Because?
<v Ira      Glass>因为？

00:39:16.640 --> 00:39:28.660
<v Mike Paterniti>Because he was going to kill himself. And because he didn't.
<v Mike Paterniti>因为他本打算自杀。而因为他没有死。

00:39:28.660 --> 00:39:33.370
<v Ira Glass>So did you feel good?
<v Ira Glass>那你当时感觉好吗？

00:39:33.370 --> 00:40:02.710
<v Mike Paterniti>Um, no. I didn't feel good. I felt like kind of nauseous. I felt like, wow, you know, they're-- every week, somebody actually does this thing. And even if we were to clone Mr. Chen, and there were 200 of them out there, they'd probably still, one a week, someone would figure out how to do it. And then, like, oh my god, who's coming next. You know?
<v Mike Paterniti>嗯，不好。我感觉有点恶心。我当时想，哇，你知道的，他们——每周真的都有人会做这种事。即使我们把陈先生克隆出200个，到处都是他们的身影，可能每周还是会发生一次，总会有人想出怎么去跳桥。然后就会觉得，天哪，下一个会是谁。你知道吗？

00:40:02.710 --> 00:40:06.160
<v Ira Glass>And so Mr. Chen comes back, right?
<v Ira Glass>接着陈先生回来了，对吧？

00:40:06.160 --> 00:40:28.630
<v Mike Paterniti>Yeah, well, it took Mr. Chen a while to come back on his moped. But when he came-- when he showed up, the crowd sort of parted. And I was holding onto this man, whose name was Fan Ping. And he said to me, step away, which I thought was a really bad idea because we're standing right next to the railing.
<v Mike Paterniti>对，嗯，陈先生骑着助力车花了一会儿才回来。但当他回来——当他出现时，人群自动分出了一条路。当时我正抓着这个名叫范平的男子。他对我说，退开，我当时觉得这实在是个极其糟糕的主意，因为我们就站在栏杆旁边。

00:40:28.630 --> 00:40:44.330
<v Mike Paterniti>But he had such command of the situation and all the nuances of the situation that I just stepped away. I just let go and stepped away. And then he said, I want to take your picture, which seemed like, you know, I didn't even understand what that was about.
<v Mike Paterniti>但他对局势以及局势的所有细微差别有着极强的掌控力，以至于我真的退开了。我放手并退了开来。然后他说，我想给你拍张照片，这感觉就像是——我当时甚至不明白这到底是怎么回事。

00:40:44.330 --> 00:40:45.730
<v Ira Glass>He's taking the picture of the guy.
<v Ira Glass>他在给那家伙拍照。

00:40:45.730 --> 00:40:46.820
<v Mike Paterniti>Yeah.
<v Mike Paterniti>对。

00:40:46.820 --> 00:40:48.050
<v Ira Glass>Mhm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯哼。

00:40:48.050 --> 00:40:57.580
<v Mike Paterniti>So he pulls out his cell phone with the camera. Takes a picture. And then he says, and now I think I should punch you in the face.
<v Mike Paterniti>于是他掏出带摄像功能的手机。拍了一张照片。接着他说，现在我觉得我应该扇你耳光。

00:40:57.580 --> 00:40:59.900
<v Ira Glass>Holy--
<v Ira Glass>天哪——

00:40:59.900 --> 00:41:22.430
<v Mike Paterniti>And then he said, you call yourself Chinese. How dare you? How dare you call yourself Chinese-- come up on this bridge with the intention of killing yourself today? You know, you are somebody's son. You know, how dare you? I'm going to punch you in the face. I'm going to punch you right now.
<v Mike Paterniti>然后他说，你自称是中国人。你怎么敢？你怎么敢自称是中国人——今天带着自杀的企图来到这座桥上？你知道的，你是某个人的儿子。你知道的，你怎么敢？我要扇你耳光。我现在就要扇你耳光。

00:41:22.430 --> 00:41:28.300
<v Mike Paterniti>And the crowd, of course, is like crushing in because they think there's going to-- they think he's going to punch them.
<v Mike Paterniti>人群当然立刻像潮水般涌了过来，因为他们以为会发生——他们以为他要扇他们耳光。

00:41:28.300 --> 00:41:32.890
<v Ira Glass>You know, I'm just sitting here, like, with my mouth open as you're saying this.
<v Ira Glass>你知道的，听你这么说，我只能目瞪口呆地坐在那里。

00:41:32.890 --> 00:41:59.360
<v Mike Paterniti>So he kind of takes another step in closer. And Fan Ping says, look, I'm only doing this because my father was in the Red Army, and he's lost all of his disability insurance. And there's no way for him to live anymore. And I'm a lousy son because I can't provide for him. And all of our documents burned in a fire. And without those documents, we can't get any help.
<v Mike Paterniti>于是他向前迈近了一步。范平说，听着，我这么做只是因为我父亲曾在红军服役，但他失去了所有的伤残保险。他再也没有办法活下去了。而我是一个糟糕的儿子，因为我无法养活他。我们所有的文件都在一场火灾中烧毁了。没有这些文件，我们就无法得到任何帮助。

00:41:59.360 --> 00:42:32.760
<v Mike Paterniti>And Mr. Chen says, there's nothing worth this, you know? There's no problem that we can't solve. And then he moves in a little bit closer, and he touches his arm. So he's sort of holding him by the elbow with his right hand. Mr. Chen says, I-- you know, I think I can help you. I don't like this. I don't like what you're doing here. This isn't the way to solve anything. And at that point, they have each other's word that they're going to meet on Monday morning at Mr. Chen's office.
<v Mike Paterniti>陈先生说，没有什么事情值得付出这样的代价，你知道吗？没有什么问题是我们解决不了的。然后他再往前走近了一点，伸手碰了碰他的手臂。所以他用右手抓着他的手肘。陈先生说，我——你知道的，我想我能帮到你。我不喜欢这样。我不喜欢你在这里做的事。这不是解决任何问题的方法。就在那个时候，他们互相承诺，星期一早上在陈先生的办公室见面。

00:42:32.760 --> 00:42:38.590
<v Ira Glass>Do you get creeped out on any big bridge now?
<v Ira Glass>你现在看到任何大桥都会觉得毛骨悚然吗？

00:42:38.590 --> 00:43:12.670
<v Mike      Paterniti>Yeah. Well, obviously, after having been on the bridge, I started looking at bridges for their suicide potential. And every bridge is that bridge in Nanjing. And every person is potentially Fan Ping. And every other person is potentially Mr. Chen. And, you know, it just-- you kind of look at it like, oh, I wonder if that is a bridge people would jump from. And I wonder if maybe someone should be out here.
<v Mike      Paterniti>会的。嗯，显然，在去过那座桥之后，我开始用自杀潜力的眼光审视各大桥梁。每一座桥都是南京的那座桥。每一个人都可能是范平。而另外每一个人都可能是陈先生。你知道的，这只会让你觉得——哦，我在想那是不是一座人们会跳下去的桥。我在想也许应该有人在那里守着。

00:43:12.670 --> 00:43:27.790
<v Ira Glass>Mike Paterniti. He first wrote about meeting Mr. Chen for GQ magazine. There's a documentary out there about Mr. Chen and the bridge called The Angel of Nanjing.
<v Ira Glass>迈克·帕特尼蒂。他最初是在《GQ》杂志上撰文讲述他与陈先生的相遇的。有一部关于陈先生和大桥的纪录片，名为《南京天使》。

00:43:27.790 --> 00:43:53.450
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, Yes in My Backyard. Now this story about some of the mysterious things happening on the edges of the city, in the shadow of the city, right under our noses. And to put this story in some context, we're going to turn now to Jorge Just. You may remember Jorge. He's done some stories for our program.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，“后院起火”。现在这个故事讲述了一些发生在城市边缘、城市阴影中、就在我们眼皮底下的神秘事件。为了让这个故事更具背景，我们现在将转向豪尔赫·胡斯特（Jorge Just）。你可能还记得豪尔赫。他为我们的节目做过一些报道。

00:43:53.450 --> 00:44:07.890
<v Ira Glass>He says that when you move to a new city, you cannot get into the regular conversations that everybody else gets into. He found this out a little while back, when he did the one thing that everyone in Chicago agrees is the very worst thing that anybody can do. He moved to New York.
<v Ira Glass>他说，当你搬到一个新城市时，你无法融入其他人都在进行的常规对话。不久前他发现了这一点，当时他做了一件芝加哥所有人都认为任何人能做的最糟糕的事。他搬到了纽约。

00:44:07.890 --> 00:44:22.310
<v Jorge Just>All New Yorkers want to talk about is what subway train to take to get from point A to point B. And it goes on and on. And you can't say anything. You can't be like, you know they discovered a tenth planet. And they would be like, well you would take the DMZ, you know?
<v Jorge Just>所有纽约人只想谈论坐哪条地铁能从A点到B点。而且没完没了。你什么也插不上话。你不能说，你知道他们发现第十颗行星了吗。他们会说，哦，那你得坐非军事区列车，你知道吗？

00:44:22.310 --> 00:44:24.050
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHING] To get to the tenth planet.
<v Ira Glass>[笑声] 去第十颗行星。

00:44:24.050 --> 00:44:49.920
<v Jorge Just>It's inescapable. And when that conversation finally peters out, it somehow-- and it doesn't fail-- turns into a conversation about cell phone reception. You can't get into the-- you can't get into the conversation. You don't know where the dead spots are. So you can't do any small talk. So what happens is the small talk becomes, oh, you just moved to New York. Where are you from? Oh, you're from Chicago. How do you like New York? How do you like New York?
<v Jorge Just>这真是无可逃避。当这个话题终于渐渐平息时，它不知怎么地——而且屡试不爽——会变成关于手机信号的对话。你根本插不上话——你插不上话。你不知道哪里是信号盲区。所以你没法进行任何闲聊。于是结果就是，闲聊变成了：哦，你刚搬到纽约。你从哪来？哦，你来自芝加哥。你觉得纽约怎么样？你觉得纽约怎么样？

00:44:49.920 --> 00:45:03.580
<v Jorge Just>Everybody wants to know how you like New York because they want you to say, New York is the greatest place that I've ever been to. And I've burned all of my connections to anywhere that I've ever been before because I love it so much here. When, in fact, people would say, so how do you like New York?
<v Jorge Just>每个人都想知道你觉得纽约怎么样，因为他们希望你说：“纽约是我去过最棒的地方。我已经烧断了我与去过的任何地方的所有联系，因为我太爱这里了。”而事实上，当人们问：“你觉得纽约怎么样？”

00:45:03.580 --> 00:45:30.480
<v Jorge Just>And you're like, well, you know, I like it. It's big and stuff, but I really like Chicago. You know? Oh really? What's Chicago like? Chicago is this wonderful dreamland where there's a bar on every corner and, you know, the bridges smell like chocolate. And then you'd pretty much have a silence. And the ice in your glass would clink a couple of times. And then they'd say, the bridges smell like chocolate?
<v Jorge Just>你会说：“嗯，你知道的，我挺喜欢。它很大之类的，但我真的很喜欢芝加哥。你知道吗？”“哦，真的吗？芝加哥是什么样的？”芝加哥是一个美妙的梦幻之地，每个街角都有一家酒吧，你知道的，桥闻起来有巧克力的味道。然后就会陷入一阵沉默。你杯子里的冰块会碰撞几下。然后他们会说：“桥闻起来有巧克力的味道？”

00:45:30.480 --> 00:45:59.850
<v Jorge Just>And then I'd describe how wonderful it is that the bridges smelled like chocolate. And this is something that people in New York have never, ever, ever, ever, ever, ever believed. But if you get up early in the morning, and it's sort of quiet out, and you go to the right bridge, and it's just that sort of magic twinkling hour, where the sun is coming up, and you're in a big city, but nobody is around, every now and again, they smell like brownies.
<v Jorge Just>然后我会描述桥闻起来有巧克力的味道是多么美妙。而这是纽约人从来、从来、从来、从来、从来、从来都不相信的事。但是如果你一大早起床，外面还算安静，你走到对的那座桥上，那正是一种神奇的闪烁时刻，太阳升起，你在一个大城市里，但周围空无一人，偶尔，它们闻起来就像布朗尼蛋糕。

00:45:59.850 --> 00:46:06.940
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. That's actually true. That's very, very true.
<v Ira Glass>是的。这其实是真的。这是非常、非常真实的。

00:46:06.940 --> 00:46:07.690
<v Jorge Just>It's true.
<v Jorge Just>这是真的。

00:46:07.690 --> 00:46:16.190
<v Ira Glass>I can say it's true. And the reason why is because there's a chocolate plant on the west side that spews the smell of chocolate.
<v Ira Glass>我可以作证这是真的。原因是因为西区有一家巧克力厂，会散发出巧克力的气味。

00:46:16.190 --> 00:46:57.750
<v Jorge Just>Yeah. The smell of magic. To say, like, the bridge smells like chocolate doesn't convey like what actually happens. What actually happens is that when you're walking across a bridge, and you're dodging cars, and it's a bridge over a dead river, in the middle of a part of town that is industrial and totally unnatural, you just sort of walk into this cloud of, like, the sweetest memory you have of cookies being made as a child. Your sweetest childhood memory. You can walk into that, and you can walk into it by surprise in the middle of the day in the middle of the city.
<v Jorge Just>是的。魔法的味道。说“桥闻起来像巧克力”并不能表达实际发生的情况。实际发生的情况是，当你走过一座桥，躲避着汽车，这是一座横跨枯竭河流的桥，位于一个工业化且完全不自然的城区中央，你就会突然走进这样一团云雾中，就像你童年制作饼干的最甜蜜的记忆。你最甜蜜的童年记忆。你可以走进那个世界，而且你会在一天的正午、城市的正中央猝不及防地走进去。

00:46:57.750 --> 00:47:01.010
<v Ira Glass>Now, you know that all this is ending, right?
<v Ira Glass>现在，你知道这一切都要结束了，对吧？

00:47:01.010 --> 00:47:04.610
<v Jorge Just>I know. I know. It's like 1,000 little stabs in the heart.
<v Jorge Just>我知道。我知道。这就像心头被扎了一千下。

00:47:04.610 --> 00:47:06.830
<v Ira Glass>Thanks to the federal government.
<v Ira Glass>多亏了联邦政府。

00:47:06.830 --> 00:47:09.740
<v Jorge Just>It's like a million little stabs in the heart.
<v Jorge Just>这就像心头被扎了一百万下。

00:47:09.740 --> 00:47:32.170
<v Ira Glass>What happened is this. Somebody complained about the chocolate smell. They complained to the Environmental Protection Agency. And the federal government, ever responsive to even a single complaint from any of its citizens anywhere in the country, leapt into action. They sent inspectors to the Blommer Chocolate Company, which has been making chocolate bars and other goodies on Chicago's west side since 1939.
<v Ira Glass>事情是这样的。有人抱怨巧克力味。他们向环境保护局进行了投诉。而联邦政府一如既往地对全国任何地方任何公民的哪怕单一投诉做出回应，迅速采取了行动。他们向布洛默巧克力公司（Blommer Chocolate Company）派出了检查员，这家公司自1939年以来一直在芝加哥西区生产巧克力棒和其他糖果。

00:47:32.170 --> 00:47:50.800
<v Ira Glass>Inspectors found that too much cocoa dust was going into the air-- more than is legal under federal standards. The plant installed filtering equipment. In fact, they say they'd been planning to get that equipment in place even before the EPA dropped by. In any case, fewer cocoa particles in the air means less delicious chocolatey aroma.
<v Ira Glass>检查员发现空气中进入了太多的可可粉尘——超过了联邦标准规定的合法限额。该工厂安装了过滤设备。事实上，他们表示，甚至在环保局光顾之前，他们就已经计划好安装这些设备了。无论如何，空气中的可可颗粒减少意味着美味的巧克力香气也随之减少。

00:47:50.800 --> 00:48:07.330
<v Brian Urbaszewski>It's kind of curious to think of, like, you know, one small chocolate factory has-- somebody complained, and they went out there looked. And, yes, there's a problem, and we're going to fix it. But yet, you know, we have thousands of times where it's happened at the power plants, and nothing's happened.
<v Brian Urbaszewski>想到这些挺让人好奇的，比如说，一家小巧克力工厂——有人抱怨了，他们就去查看了。然后，是的，这里有个问题，我们要解决它。然而，你知道，我们在发电厂发生了成千上万次这种情况，却什么也没发生。

00:48:07.330 --> 00:48:30.470
<v Ira Glass>That's Brian Urbaszewski, Director of Environmental Health Programs for the American Lung Association in Chicago. And as he points out-- it has been widely reported here-- the Illinois Attorney General's office has documented over 7,600 violations, similar to the chocolate company violation, at six coal plants in Illinois in the last six years. And the EPA has never gone after any of those coal plants.
<v Ira Glass>那是芝加哥美国肺脏协会环境健康项目总监布莱恩·乌尔巴舍夫斯基（Brian Urbaszewski）。正如他所指出的——这里已经有广泛报道——伊利诺伊州总检察长办公室在过去的六年里，在伊利诺伊州的六座燃煤电厂记录了7600多起违规行为，这与巧克力公司的违规行为类似。而环保局从未对这些燃煤电厂采取过任何行动。

00:48:30.470 --> 00:48:41.250
<v Brian Urbaszewski>OK. Let's step back a minute, because chocolate factories are not a major source of this fine particle pollution. When you look at power plants, they're responsible for about a quarter of the problem.
<v Brian Urbaszewski>好的。让我们退一步说，因为巧克力工厂并不是这种微细颗粒污染的主要来源。当你看看发电厂时，它们造成了大约四分之一的问题。

00:48:41.250 --> 00:48:45.500
<v Ira Glass>And chocolate? Is chocolate a quarter of the problem as well?
<v Ira Glass>那巧克力呢？巧克力也造成了四分之一的问题吗？

00:48:45.500 --> 00:48:51.080
<v Brian Urbaszewski>No, no, no, no. It's probably far, far, far less than 1%.
<v Brian Urbaszewski>不，不，不，不。它可能远远、远远、远远不到1%。

00:48:51.080 --> 00:49:01.940
<v Ira Glass>Oh. Now, there's a quote that you gave where you used an animal metaphor that I've seen quoted widely in a million articles that I just would like you to repeat here for our listeners.
<v Ira Glass>哦。现在，你曾说过一段话，里面用了一个动物隐喻，我在一百万篇文章中都看到过这个引用的广泛传播，我只是想请你在这里为我们的听众重复一遍。

00:49:01.940 --> 00:49:04.730
<v Brian Urbaszewski>Oh, I don't know if I can. Actually, if this is the wolves?
<v Brian Urbaszewski>哦，我不知道我能不能。其实，这是指狼吗？

00:49:04.730 --> 00:49:05.690
<v Ira Glass>Mhm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯哼。

00:49:05.690 --> 00:49:07.090
<v Brian Urbaszewski>And the ant thing?
<v Brian Urbaszewski>还有蚂蚁的那件事？

00:49:07.090 --> 00:49:08.490
<v Ira Glass>I'm afraid so.
<v Ira Glass>恐怕是的。

00:49:08.490 --> 00:49:14.550
<v Brian Urbaszewski>I'va actually got San Francisco animal activists after me for that thing, saying that wolves are not dangerous to humans.
<v Brian Urbaszewski>我其实因为那件事遭到了旧金山动物活动人士的纠缠，他们说狼对人类并不危险。

00:49:14.550 --> 00:49:15.050
<v Brian Urbaszewski>[LAUGHTER]
<v Brian Urbaszewski>[笑声]

00:49:14.940 --> 00:49:16.910
<v Ira Glass>That being said--
<v Ira Glass>话虽如此——

00:49:16.910 --> 00:49:20.400
<v Brian Urbaszewski>Um-- [SIGH] I--
<v Brian Urbaszewski>嗯——[叹气] 我——

00:49:20.400 --> 00:49:42.970
<v Ira       Glass>Well, I'll say it if you don't feel like you can. You said that-- hold on for a second. I have it here. You said the EPA-- what the EPA was doing with this chocolate factory and ignoring the coal plants-- you said, quote, "It's like crushing an ant when there's a pack of wolves around, then claiming you have saved people from harm."
<v Ira       Glass>好吧，如果你觉得不方便说，那我来代劳。你说过——等一下。我有记录。你说环保局——环保局对这家巧克力工厂的做法以及对燃煤电厂的忽视——你说过，“这就像在一群狼周围踩死一只蚂蚁，然后声称你已经拯救了人们免受伤害。”

00:49:42.970 --> 00:49:48.540
<v Brian Urbaszewski>How about if we say-- all right-- you know-- it's like crushing an ant-- well, see.
<v Brian Urbaszewski>如果我们这么说怎么样——好吧——你知道的——这就像踩死一只蚂蚁——好吧，你看。

00:49:48.540 --> 00:49:52.870
<v Ira Glass>Don't be scared of those animal rights people.
<v Ira Glass>别怕那些动物权益保护人士。

00:49:52.870 --> 00:49:56.000
<v Brian Urbaszewski>No, no. I'm just trying to think, I'm going to use sharks instead. Nobody likes sharks.
<v Brian Urbaszewski>不，不。我只是在想要不改用鲨鱼好了。没人喜欢鲨鱼。

00:49:56.000 --> 00:50:06.740
<v Ira Glass>I just feel like this is-- just like my entire relationship to government right now can be summed up by this story, OK? There's all these things that are throwing particles in the air. And the only one I like is the one they're getting rid of.
<v Ira Glass>我只是觉得这——就像我对政府的整个关系现在都可以通过这个故事来总结，好吗？有那么多东西在向空气中排放颗粒物。而我唯一喜欢的那个却是他们要摆脱的那个。

00:50:06.740 --> 00:50:07.580
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHTER]
<v Ira Glass>[笑声]

00:50:07.580 --> 00:50:10.720
<v Brian Urbaszewski>Yeah. And, you know, that's my frustration as well.
<v Brian Urbaszewski>是的。而且，你知道，这也是我的挫败感所在。

00:50:10.720 --> 00:50:29.970
<v Ira Glass>The federal EPA hasn't been talking to the press about the chocolate factory. When I called the Illinois state EPA, the Manager of Compliance and Enforcement for the Bureau of Air-- a cheerful public servant named Julie Armitage-- informed me that there has been a misunderstanding. Yes, she said, the coal plants had belched out too many particles 7,600 times.
<v Ira Glass>联邦环保局没有就巧克力工厂一事接受媒体采访。当我打电话给伊利诺伊州环保局时，空气局合规与执法经理——一位名叫朱莉·阿米蒂奇（Julie Armitage）的快乐公务员——告知我这是一个误解。是的，她说，燃煤电厂曾7600次喷出过多的颗粒物。

00:50:29.970 --> 00:50:54.350
<v Ira Glass>But you see, these times was very, very short. At the least, a momentary spike. At the most, six minutes long. Each one was a blip, she said. Automatic monitoring equipment is going 24 hours a day taking readings. Add up all the blips per year, and you get 211 blips per plant per year, meaning that well over 99% of the time, the plants are in compliance with the law.
<v Ira Glass>但你看，这些次数非常、非常短。最短的只是一次瞬间的峰值。最长的也就六分钟。她说，每一次都只是一个小波段。自动监测设备全天24小时进行读数。把每年所有的波段加起来，每个电厂每年有211个波段，这意味着在99%以上的时间里，这些电厂都符合法律规定。

00:50:54.350 --> 00:51:05.790
<v Julie Armitage>Yes. Taken out of context, it appears to be a very bad situation. Put into context, it's virtually a non-issue.
<v Julie Armitage>是的。脱离了上下文，这似乎是一个非常糟糕的情况。放到上下文中来看，这几乎根本不是个问题。

00:51:05.790 --> 00:51:10.300
<v Ira Glass>And as for the fact that now there may be less chocolate smell in Chicago?
<v Ira Glass>至于芝加哥现在的巧克力味可能会减少的事实呢？

00:51:10.300 --> 00:51:20.970
<v Julie Armitage>You know, [LAUGHING] I'm not really in a position. Would I prefer to not have had the hullabaloo that broke loose? Yes.
<v Julie Armitage>你知道，[笑声] 我其实不便置评。我宁愿没有爆发这场轩然大波吗？是的。

00:51:20.970 --> 00:51:29.590
<v Ira Glass>And you don't feel any sort of twinge-- as an environmental regulator who's here to make our world a better place, as you are-- that that could be the upshot of the whole thing?
<v Ira Glass>作为一个旨在让我们的世界变得更美好的环境监管机构，你难道没有感到一丝内疚——这也是整个事件的最终结果吗？

00:51:29.590 --> 00:51:32.050
<v Julie Armitage>That the chocolate aroma disappears?
<v Julie Armitage>巧克力香味消失了？

00:51:32.050 --> 00:51:36.250
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. You don't feel any sort of twinge if that were to happen?
<v Ira Glass>是的。如果真的发生这种情况，你难道没有一丝内疚感吗？

00:51:36.250 --> 00:51:46.780
<v Julie Armitage>Well, you know, unfortunately, my job here is to ensure compliance with environmental laws and regulations. And-- and--
<v Julie Armitage>嗯，你知道，不幸的是，我在这里的工作是确保遵守环境法律和法规。而且——而且——

00:51:46.780 --> 00:51:52.460
<v Ira Glass>Wherever this sentence is going, this is exactly not the answer we the people of Illinois want to hear.
<v Ira Glass>不管这句话接下来要说什么，这绝对不是我们伊利诺伊州人民想听到的答案。

00:51:52.460 --> 00:51:53.160
<v Julie Armitage>Well--
<v Julie Armitage>好吧——

00:51:53.160 --> 00:51:55.120
<v Ira Glass>We don't want to hear about laws and regulations.
<v Ira Glass>我们不想听什么法律法规。

00:51:55.120 --> 00:51:59.080
<v Julie Armitage>Well, but you know, they're there for a reason. And for the most part, you know,
<v Julie Armitage>嗯，但是你知道，它们存在是有原因的。而且在大多数情况下，你知道，

00:51:59.080 --> 00:52:29.350
<v Ira Glass>Everybody was following the rules, she says. The Feds inspected, just like they're supposed to. Blommers was, in fact, emitting too much chocolate. End of story. And then in the months after I had that conversation with her, the EPA says Blommer fixed the problem, stopped spewing particles into the air that violated the law. And good news, incredibly, what they're emitting still smells like delicious chocolate.
<v Ira Glass>每个人都在遵守规则，她说。联邦机构进行了检查，就像他们应该做的那样。事实上，布洛默公司确实排放了过多的巧克力。故事结束。在我与她对话后的几个月里，环保局表示布洛默公司已经解决了这个问题，停止向空气中排放违法的颗粒物。好消息是，令人难以置信的是，他们排放的东西闻起来依然像美味的巧克力。

00:52:29.350 --> 00:53:05.740
<v Ira Glass>Well, today's episode of our show was produced by Diane Cook, Robyn Semien and myself, with Alex Blumberg, Jane Marie, Sarah Koenig, Amy O'Leary, and Lisa Pollak. Our senior producer for this episode was Julie Snyder. Production help from Sam Hallgren, Thea Chaloner, Seth Lind, Tommy Andreas, and B.A. Parker. Matt Tierney is our technical director. Music help from Jessica Hopper.
<v Ira Glass>好的，我们今天的节目由黛安·库克（Diane Cook）、罗宾·瑟米安（Robyn Semien）和我本人制作，参与制作的还有亚历克斯·布隆伯格（Alex Blumberg）、简·玛丽（Jane Marie）、萨拉·科尼格（Sarah Koenig）、艾米·奥利里（Amy O'Leary）和莉莎·波拉克（Lisa Pollak）。我们本期节目的资深制作人是朱莉·斯奈德（Julie Snyder）。山姆·霍尔格伦（Sam Hallgren）、西娅·沙洛纳（Thea Chaloner）、塞斯·林德（Seth Lind）、汤米·安德烈亚斯（Tommy Andreas）和B.A.帕克（B.A. Parker）提供了制作协助。马特·蒂尔尼（Matt Tierney）是我们的技术总监。杰西卡·霍珀（Jessica Hopper）提供了音乐协助。

00:53:05.740 --> 00:53:22.340
<v Ira Glass>Our website, thisamericanlife.org. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Thanks, as always, to our program's co-founder Mr. Torey Malatia, who asked me to tell you he can kick the ass of anybody in Manhattan, Brooklyn, Queens, the Bronx--
<v Ira Glass>我们的网站是thisamericanlife.org。《This American Life》由公共广播交换中心（PRX）分发给公共广播电台。一如既往，感谢我们节目的联合创始人托雷·马拉蒂亚（Torey Malatia）先生，他让我告诉你，他可以痛打曼哈顿、布鲁克林、皇后区、布朗克斯的任何人——

00:53:22.340 --> 00:53:29.570
<v Jorge Just>And this is something that people in New York have never, ever, ever, ever, ever, ever, ever believed.
